// Auto-generated offline chapter bundle for Junior Coders Book
window.BOOK_PYTHON_CHAPTERS = {
  "part-01-python-foundations/ch01-welcome-to-python.md": "# Chapter 1: Welcome to Python — The Language of AI 🐍\r\n\r\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\r\n\r\nA new student joins your class and asks the way to the library. You could give a long, confusing speech. Or you could say: \"Go down the stairs. Turn left. It is the second door.\" Short, clear steps are easy to follow. Python is a programming language built on that idea. Its instructions are short and read almost like plain English.\r\n\r\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\r\n\r\n- Explain what Python is and why scientists and AI engineers use it.\r\n- Write and run your first program with `print()`.\r\n- Add comments that explain your code to other people.\r\n- Use indentation (spaces at the start of a line) to group code.\r\n- Describe how the Python interpreter runs a program line by line.\r\n\r\n## 💡 Why Python?\r\n\r\nImagine you want to tell a friend how to make tea:\r\n\r\n- In some computer languages, you write: *\"Initialize hot water vessel, create tea leaf object, attach event listener for boiling point, allocate memory for mug...\"*\r\n- In **Python**, you write: *\"Boil water. Add tea. Serve.\"*\r\n\r\nPython was created by Guido van Rossum and first released in 1991. Today we use **Python 3**. Its main idea is simple: **code is read much more often than it is written.** So Python code should be easy to read.\r\n\r\nThat is why scientists, doctors, space engineers and AI researchers use Python. They can focus on their problem instead of on tricky punctuation.\r\n\r\n```\r\nOther languages: lots of { } ; and setup\r\nPython:          clean lines, plain words\r\n```\r\n\r\n| Python is used for… | Example |\r\n| :--- | :--- |\r\n| Data science | Studying rainfall records |\r\n| Machine learning (AI) | Sorting healthy and sick plant leaves |\r\n| Automation | Renaming 500 homework files at once |\r\n| Web servers | Running a school library website |\r\n\r\n## ⚡ Your Very First Python Program\r\n\r\nEvery programmer begins with a greeting. In Python, one line is enough:\r\n\r\n```python\r\nprint(\"Bismillah! Welcome to Python.\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nBismillah! Welcome to Python.\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Breaking Down the Line\r\n\r\n1. `print` is a **function**: a named action the computer already knows how to do.\r\n2. The round brackets `()` hold the **argument**: the information you give the function.\r\n3. The quotation marks `\" \"` mark a **string**: a piece of text.\r\n4. There is **no semicolon** `;` at the end. Python knows a command ends when the line ends.\r\n\r\n### Printing More Than One Thing\r\n\r\nYou can call `print()` many times. Each call prints a new line. You can also give `print()` several items separated by commas. Python puts a space between them.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nprint(\"Junior Coders Science Club\")\r\nprint(\"Members:\", 24)\r\nprint(\"Meeting day:\", \"Thursday\", \"after school\")\r\nprint()\r\nprint(\"Numbers need no quotes:\", 7 + 5)\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nJunior Coders Science Club\r\nMembers: 24\r\nMeeting day: Thursday after school\r\n\r\nNumbers need no quotes: 12\r\n```\r\n\r\nNotice three things. `print()` with nothing inside prints an empty line. Numbers do not need quotes. And `7 + 5` was worked out before printing, so we see `12`.\r\n\r\n## 📝 Comments: Notes for Humans\r\n\r\nA line that starts with `#` is a **comment**. Python skips it completely. Comments explain *why* the code does something. They help your teacher, your classmates and your future self.\r\n\r\n```python\r\n# This program prints the club timetable\r\nprint(\"Robotics: Monday\")    # a comment can sit after code too\r\n# print(\"This line is switched off\")\r\nprint(\"Astronomy: Wednesday\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nRobotics: Monday\r\nAstronomy: Wednesday\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe third line did not print. Putting `#` in front of code is a handy way to switch it off while testing.\r\n\r\n## 🧱 The Power of Indentation\r\n\r\nIn JavaScript or PHP, a group of lines is wrapped in curly braces `{ }`:\r\n\r\n```javascript\r\n// JavaScript\r\nif (isSunny) {\r\n    console.log(\"Go for a morning walk!\");\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nPython does **not** use curly braces. It uses **indentation**: spaces at the start of a line. Lines with the same indentation belong to the same group. (The `if` keyword makes a decision. You will learn it properly in Chapter 4.)\r\n\r\n```python\r\nis_sunny = True\r\n\r\nif is_sunny:\r\n    print(\"Go for a morning walk!\")\r\n    print(\"Breathe fresh air and be grateful.\")\r\nprint(\"This line always runs.\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nGo for a morning walk!\r\nBreathe fresh air and be grateful.\r\nThis line always runs.\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe two indented lines belong to the `if`. The last line is not indented, so it runs no matter what.\r\n\r\n> ⚠️ **Golden Rule**: use **4 spaces** for each level of indentation. Neat, consistent code is part of *Iḥsān*: doing work with care.\r\n\r\n## 🛠️ How Python Runs: The Interpreter\r\n\r\nPython is an **interpreted** language. An **interpreter** is a program that reads your code and runs it straight away, one line at a time.\r\n\r\n```\r\n[hello.py] --> [Interpreter] --> [Output]\r\n```\r\n\r\nOn a computer with Python installed, you save your code in a file ending in `.py` and type:\r\n\r\n```bash\r\npython hello.py\r\n```\r\n\r\nOn this website, you can type code into the **Python Console** studio and press Run. It works in your browser.\r\n\r\nBecause Python goes line by line, earlier lines run before a mistake is found. Here, line 3 has a spelling mistake:\r\n\r\n```python\r\nprint(\"Step 1: open the lab\")\r\nprint(\"Step 2: check the sensors\")\r\npirnt(\"Step 3: write the report\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nStep 1: open the lab\r\nStep 2: check the sensors\r\nNameError: name 'pirnt' is not defined. Did you mean: 'print'?\r\n```\r\n\r\nLines 1 and 2 printed. Then Python stopped at line 3. A `NameError` means Python does not know the name `pirnt`. It even suggests the right word.\r\n\r\n## 🔮 Predict the Output\r\n\r\nRead this code. Decide what it prints before you run it.\r\n\r\n```python\r\n# Library opening times\r\nprint(\"Library\")\r\nprint(\"Open from\", 8, \"to\", 4)\r\n# print(\"Closed on Friday\")\r\nprint(10 + 20)\r\n```\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```text\r\nLibrary\r\nOpen from 8 to 4\r\n30\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe comment line does not run. `10 + 20` is calculated first.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\r\n\r\n**Forgetting the quotes around text.** Without quotes, Python thinks the word is the name of something it should already know.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nprint(Hello)\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nNameError: name 'Hello' is not defined\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Fixed version:** put text inside quotes.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nprint(\"Hello\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nHello\r\n```\r\n\r\nA second common slip is a capital letter. `Print(\"Hi\")` fails too, because Python is **case-sensitive**: `print` and `Print` are different names.\r\n\r\n## ✍️ Try It\r\n\r\nOpen the Python Console (or Python on your computer). Write a 3-line program that introduces you:\r\n\r\n```python\r\nprint(\"My name is Tariq.\")\r\nprint(\"I am learning Python to build helpful tools.\")\r\nprint(\"Seeking beneficial knowledge is my goal!\")\r\n```\r\n\r\nChange the name and the sentences to your own. **Hint:** every line needs `print`, round brackets and quotes.\r\n\r\n## 📝 Recap\r\n\r\n- Python is a readable language used in science, data and AI.\r\n- `print()` shows text or numbers on the screen. Text goes in quotes.\r\n- `#` starts a comment. Python ignores comments.\r\n- Python groups lines with indentation, not curly braces. Use 4 spaces.\r\n- The interpreter runs code line by line and stops at the first error.\r\n\r\n## 🏆 Practice\r\n\r\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\r\nWhat does Python use instead of curly braces `{ }` to group lines of code? How many spaces is the standard?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\nPython uses **indentation** (spaces at the start of a line). The standard is **4 spaces** per level.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\r\nThis program has two mistakes. Find and fix them.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nPrint(\"School clean-up day\")\r\nprint(Saturday at 9)\r\n```\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n`Print` must be lowercase, and the text needs quotes:\r\n\r\n```python\r\nprint(\"School clean-up day\")\r\nprint(\"Saturday at 9\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\r\nMake a small notice board for a science fair.\r\n\r\n1. Add a comment on the first line saying what the program does.\r\n2. Print a title line.\r\n3. Print the number of projects using a sum, such as `12 + 9`.\r\n4. Print an empty line, then a thank-you message.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```python\r\n# Science fair notice board\r\nprint(\"Junior Coders Science Fair\")\r\nprint(\"Projects on show:\", 12 + 9)\r\nprint()\r\nprint(\"Thank you to all our volunteers!\")\r\n```\r\n\r\nOutput:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nJunior Coders Science Fair\r\nProjects on show: 21\r\n\r\nThank you to all our volunteers!\r\n```\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n",
  "part-01-python-foundations/ch02-variables-and-data-types.md": "# Chapter 2: Variables & Data Types 📦\r\n\r\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\r\n\r\nThe school pantry has many jars on a shelf. Each jar has a label: \"Rice\", \"Lentils\", \"Dates\". When the teacher says \"bring the lentils\", you find the jar by reading its label. You do not need to know which shelf it is on. A Python variable works the same way. It is a name label that lets you find a value in the computer's memory.\r\n\r\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\r\n\r\n- Create variables and change their values.\r\n- Recognise the four basic data types: `int`, `float`, `str` and `bool`.\r\n- Check a value's type with `type()`.\r\n- Calculate with Python's arithmetic operators.\r\n- Convert values from one type to another, and round decimals with `round()`.\r\n\r\n## 💡 Variables Are Name Labels\r\n\r\nA **variable** is a name that points to a value. Think of it as a **sticky label** attached to a value in memory.\r\n\r\n```\r\n Name (label)         Value in memory\r\n┌──────────────┐     ┌──────────┐\r\n│ student_name │ ──> │ \"Fatima\" │\r\n└──────────────┘     └──────────┘\r\n```\r\n\r\nYou create a variable by writing a name, then `=`, then a value. The `=` sign means **\"store this value under this name\"**. It is called **assignment**. In Python you do not write `var`, `let` or a type. Python works out the type for you.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nbook_title = \"The Ethical Programmer\"\r\ncopies_available = 7\r\nprice = 14.50\r\nis_in_stock = True\r\n\r\nprint(book_title)\r\nprint(copies_available)\r\nprint(price)\r\nprint(is_in_stock)\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nThe Ethical Programmer\r\n7\r\n14.5\r\nTrue\r\n```\r\n\r\nLook at the price. We typed `14.50`, but Python printed `14.5`. The value is the same. Python simply drops the extra zero at the end.\r\n\r\n### Changing a Variable\r\n\r\nA variable can be given a new value at any time. The old value is replaced.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nbooks_donated = 12\r\nprint(\"Morning:\", books_donated)\r\n\r\nbooks_donated = 20\r\nprint(\"Afternoon:\", books_donated)\r\n\r\nbooks_donated = books_donated + 5\r\nprint(\"Evening:\", books_donated)\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nMorning: 12\r\nAfternoon: 20\r\nEvening: 25\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe last assignment reads the old value (`20`), adds `5`, and stores the new value (`25`). Python always works out the right side of `=` first.\r\n\r\n## 🗂️ The Four Basic Data Types\r\n\r\nEvery value belongs to a family called a **data type**. The type decides what you can do with the value.\r\n\r\n| Data Type | Python Name | What it holds | Examples |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| Integer | `int` | Whole numbers, positive, negative or zero | `42`, `-10`, `0` |\r\n| Float | `float` | Numbers with a decimal point | `3.14`, `99.5` |\r\n| String | `str` | Text inside single `'` or double `\"` quotes | `\"Kindness\"`, `'Cairo'` |\r\n| Boolean | `bool` | Only `True` or `False` | `True`, `False` |\r\n\r\nNote that `True` and `False` start with a capital letter and have no quotes.\r\n\r\n## 🔍 Checking Types with `type()`\r\n\r\nThe built-in function `type()` tells you the data type of any value:\r\n\r\n```python\r\ndonation_amount = 250\r\ndonor_city = \"Istanbul\"\r\nrainfall_mm = 12.5\r\nis_verified = True\r\n\r\nprint(type(donation_amount))\r\nprint(type(donor_city))\r\nprint(type(rainfall_mm))\r\nprint(type(is_verified))\r\nprint(type(\"250\"))\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\n<class 'int'>\r\n<class 'str'>\r\n<class 'float'>\r\n<class 'bool'>\r\n<class 'str'>\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe last line is a surprise for many beginners. `\"250\"` is in quotes, so it is a **string**, not a number. Quotes always make text.\r\n\r\n## 🧮 Arithmetic Operators\r\n\r\nComputers are excellent at fast, exact calculation. Here are Python's arithmetic **operators** (symbols that do maths):\r\n\r\n| Operator | Meaning | Example | Result |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| `+` | Add | `15 + 4` | `19` |\r\n| `-` | Subtract | `15 - 4` | `11` |\r\n| `*` | Multiply | `15 * 4` | `60` |\r\n| `/` | Divide (always gives a float) | `15 / 4` | `3.75` |\r\n| `//` | Floor division (whole part only) | `15 // 4` | `3` |\r\n| `%` | Remainder (also called modulo) | `15 % 4` | `3` |\r\n| `**` | Power | `2 ** 8` | `256` |\r\n\r\n```python\r\na = 15\r\nb = 4\r\n\r\nprint(a + b)\r\nprint(a - b)\r\nprint(a * b)\r\nprint(a / b)\r\nprint(a // b)\r\nprint(a % b)\r\nprint(2 ** 8)\r\nprint(10 / 2)\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\n19\r\n11\r\n60\r\n3.75\r\n3\r\n3\r\n256\r\n5.0\r\n```\r\n\r\nNotice the last line. `10 / 2` gives `5.0`, not `5`. The `/` operator always gives a float, even when the answer is whole.\r\n\r\n### Order of Operations\r\n\r\nPython follows the same order as your maths lessons. Powers come first, then `*` and `/`, then `+` and `-`. Use brackets to change the order.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nprint(2 + 3 * 4)\r\nprint((2 + 3) * 4)\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\n14\r\n20\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Short Ways to Update\r\n\r\nAdding to a running total is so common that Python has a shortcut. `total += 5` means `total = total + 5`. There is also `-=`, `*=` and `/=`.\r\n\r\n```python\r\ntrees_planted = 30\r\ntrees_planted += 12\r\nprint(trees_planted)\r\ntrees_planted -= 2\r\nprint(trees_planted)\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\n42\r\n40\r\n```\r\n\r\n## 🧺 Worked Example: The Charity Pantry Calculator\r\n\r\nA community relief drive collects food. Volunteers want to share it fairly between 25 families. The `//` operator gives each family's whole share. The `%` operator shows what is left over.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nrice_bags = 120\r\nlentil_bags = 85\r\noil_bottles = 60\r\nfamilies = 25\r\n\r\ntotal_items = rice_bags + lentil_bags + oil_bottles\r\nitems_per_family = total_items // families\r\nitems_left = total_items % families\r\n\r\nprint(\"Total items collected:\", total_items)\r\nprint(\"Items per family:\", items_per_family)\r\nprint(\"Kept in storage for emergencies:\", items_left)\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nTotal items collected: 265\r\nItems per family: 10\r\nKept in storage for emergencies: 15\r\n```\r\n\r\nCheck it: 25 × 10 = 250, and 265 − 250 = 15. The program is right.\r\n\r\n## 🔄 Type Conversion\r\n\r\nSometimes a number is stored as text, for example `\"120\"`. You must convert it before you can do maths. Converting a value to another type is called **type conversion** (or **casting**).\r\n\r\n| Function | Converts to | Example | Result |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| `int()` | Whole number | `int(\"120\")` | `120` |\r\n| `float()` | Decimal number | `float(\"2.5\")` | `2.5` |\r\n| `str()` | Text | `str(98)` | `\"98\"` |\r\n\r\n```python\r\nvisitors_text = \"120\"\r\nvisitors = int(visitors_text)\r\nprint(visitors + 5)\r\n\r\nlength = float(15)\r\nprint(length)\r\n\r\nscore = 98\r\nmessage = \"Final score: \" + str(score)\r\nprint(message)\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\n125\r\n15.0\r\nFinal score: 98\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe `+` sign joins two strings together. Joining text is called **concatenation**. That is why we turned `98` into the string `\"98\"` first.\r\n\r\n### Rounding with `round()`\r\n\r\nDivision often gives long decimals. `round(value, places)` rounds to the number of decimal places you choose.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nwater_litres = 10 / 3\r\nprint(water_litres)\r\nprint(round(water_litres, 2))\r\nprint(round(water_litres))\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\n3.3333333333333335\r\n3.33\r\n3\r\n```\r\n\r\nWith no second number, `round()` gives a whole number.\r\n\r\n## 🏷️ Naming Rules and Good Habits\r\n\r\nPython has a few strict rules for names, and some good habits (from the style guide called **PEP 8**):\r\n\r\n| Name | OK? | Why |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| `water_level` | ✅ | snake_case: lowercase words joined by `_` |\r\n| `total_donations` | ✅ | clear meaning |\r\n| `1st_student` | ❌ | cannot start with a number |\r\n| `first student` | ❌ | no spaces allowed |\r\n| `x` | ⚠️ | allowed, but tells the reader nothing |\r\n\r\nNames are **case-sensitive**: `Score` and `score` are two different variables. Choose meaningful names like `patient_temperature` or `parcel_weight`. Clear names are a kindness to the next reader (*Iḥsān*).\r\n\r\n## 🔮 Predict the Output\r\n\r\n```python\r\npages = 40\r\npages = pages * 2\r\nprint(pages // 3, pages % 3)\r\nprint(type(pages / 2))\r\n```\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```text\r\n26 2\r\n<class 'float'>\r\n```\r\n\r\n`pages` becomes 80. 80 // 3 = 26, remainder 2. Dividing with `/` always gives a float.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\r\n\r\n**Adding a number to a number stored as text.**\r\n\r\n```python\r\nvisitors_text = \"120\"\r\nprint(visitors_text + 5)\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nTypeError: can only concatenate str (not \"int\") to str\r\n```\r\n\r\nA `TypeError` means the two values are of types that cannot be combined. Python will not guess whether you wanted maths or joined text.\r\n\r\n**Fixed version:** convert the text first.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nvisitors_text = \"120\"\r\nprint(int(visitors_text) + 5)\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\n125\r\n```\r\n\r\n## ✍️ Try It\r\n\r\nA school bus holds 42 students. 190 students are going to the planetarium. Create variables `students` and `seats_per_bus`. Print how many full buses are needed and how many students are left for one extra bus.\r\n\r\n**Hint:** use `//` for full buses and `%` for the students left over. (You should get 4 full buses and 22 extra students.)\r\n\r\n## 📝 Recap\r\n\r\n- A variable is a name label for a value. `=` stores a value under a name.\r\n- The four basic types are `int`, `float`, `str` and `bool`. `type()` shows which one you have.\r\n- `/` always gives a float. `//` gives the whole part, `%` gives the remainder.\r\n- `int()`, `float()` and `str()` convert between types. `round()` tidies decimals.\r\n- Use clear snake_case names. Names are case-sensitive.\r\n\r\n## 🏆 Practice\r\n\r\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\r\nWhat is the data type of each value? `7`, `7.0`, `\"7\"`, `False`\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n`7` is `int`, `7.0` is `float`, `\"7\"` is `str` (it has quotes), `False` is `bool`.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\r\nA library has 365 books to spread across 8 shelves. Write code that prints how many books go on each shelf and how many are left over.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```python\r\nbooks = 365\r\nshelves = 8\r\nprint(\"Books per shelf:\", books // shelves)\r\nprint(\"Left over:\", books % shelves)\r\n```\r\n\r\nOutput:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nBooks per shelf: 45\r\nLeft over: 5\r\n```\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\r\nBuild a solar panel energy calculator.\r\n\r\n1. Store the number of panels (`12`) and the energy each panel makes per day (`1.75` kWh).\r\n2. Calculate the total energy per day.\r\n3. Calculate the energy for a 7-day week.\r\n4. Print both values, rounded to 1 decimal place, with a label.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```python\r\npanels = 12\r\nkwh_per_panel = 1.75\r\n\r\ndaily_kwh = panels * kwh_per_panel\r\nweekly_kwh = daily_kwh * 7\r\n\r\nprint(\"Energy per day (kWh):\", round(daily_kwh, 1))\r\nprint(\"Energy per week (kWh):\", round(weekly_kwh, 1))\r\n```\r\n\r\nOutput:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nEnergy per day (kWh): 21.0\r\nEnergy per week (kWh): 147.0\r\n```\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n",
  "part-01-python-foundations/ch03-user-input-and-formatting.md": "# Chapter 3: User Input & String Formatting (F-Strings) 🗣️\r\n\r\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\r\n\r\nAt the school library desk, the librarian has a printed card: \"Dear ______, your book is due on ______.\" She asks your name and the date, then writes them in the gaps. The card stays the same. Only the gaps change. In this chapter, Python learns to ask questions with `input()` and to fill gaps in sentences with **f-strings**.\r\n\r\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\r\n\r\n- Ask the user a question with `input()` and know that the answer is always text.\r\n- Convert typed answers into numbers with `int()` and `float()`.\r\n- Build clear sentences with f-strings.\r\n- Clean and change text with string methods such as `.strip()` and `.title()`.\r\n\r\n## 📥 Getting Input with `input()`\r\n\r\nThe `input()` function shows a question, then **pauses** the program. It waits for the person to type an answer and press <kbd>Enter</kbd>. The answer is stored in a variable.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nstudent_name = input(\"Please enter your name: \")\r\nprint(\"Peace be upon you, \" + student_name + \"!\")\r\n```\r\n\r\nWhen you run this on a computer, the screen looks like this. The person typed `Maryam`:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nPlease enter your name: Maryam\r\nPeace be upon you, Maryam!\r\n```\r\n\r\n> 💻 **Where `input()` works**: `input()` works when you run Python on a computer (for example `python greet.py` in a terminal). It does **not** work in the Python Console on this website. So in this book, we put the value straight into the code instead, like this:\r\n\r\n```python\r\nstudent_name = \"Maryam\"   # on a computer: input(\"Please enter your name: \")\r\nprint(\"Peace be upon you, \" + student_name + \"!\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nPeace be upon you, Maryam!\r\n```\r\n\r\nBoth programs print the same message. The only difference is where the name comes from.\r\n\r\n### `input()` Always Gives a String\r\n\r\nThis is the most important rule in this chapter. `input()` **always returns a string**, even when the person types digits. If they type `15`, Python receives the text `\"15\"`. To do maths, convert it first:\r\n\r\n```python\r\ndaily_pages = int(input(\"How many pages did you read today? \"))\r\nweekly_pages = daily_pages * 7\r\nprint(\"At this rate, you will read\", weekly_pages, \"pages this week!\")\r\n```\r\n\r\nIn the console version, we write the text `\"12\"` to act like a typed answer:\r\n\r\n```python\r\ntyped_answer = \"12\"            # what input() would give us\r\ndaily_pages = int(typed_answer)\r\nweekly_pages = daily_pages * 7\r\nprint(\"At this rate, you will read\", weekly_pages, \"pages this week!\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nAt this rate, you will read 84 pages this week!\r\n```\r\n\r\nUse `int()` for whole numbers and `float()` for decimals such as `36.6`.\r\n\r\n## 💎 F-Strings: Filling the Gaps\r\n\r\nJoining text with `+` works, but it gets messy. You must add spaces yourself, and numbers need `str()`:\r\n\r\n```python\r\nname = \"Zayd\"\r\nscore = 95\r\nprint(\"Student \" + name + \" scored \" + str(score) + \" out of 100.\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nStudent Zayd scored 95 out of 100.\r\n```\r\n\r\nAn **f-string** (formatted string) is easier. Put the letter `f` just before the opening quote. Then write any variable inside curly braces `{ }`. Python fills the gap with its value.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nname = \"Zayd\"\r\nscore = 95\r\nprint(f\"Student {name} scored {score} out of 100.\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nStudent Zayd scored 95 out of 100.\r\n```\r\n\r\nSame output, much easier to read. F-strings handle numbers for you, so `str()` is not needed.\r\n\r\n### Calculations Inside the Braces\r\n\r\nYou can put a small calculation inside the braces. Python works it out first.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nloaf_price = 1.25\r\nloaves = 6\r\nprint(f\"{loaves} loaves cost {loaf_price * loaves} in total.\")\r\nprint(f\"Half of the order is {loaves / 2} loaves.\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\n6 loaves cost 7.5 in total.\r\nHalf of the order is 3.0 loaves.\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Tidy Decimals with `round()`\r\n\r\nSome answers have long decimals. Use `round()` inside the braces to keep them short.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nwater_litres = 20\r\nstudents = 3\r\nshare = water_litres / students\r\nprint(f\"Each student carries {share} litres.\")\r\nprint(f\"Each student carries {round(share, 2)} litres.\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nEach student carries 6.666666666666667 litres.\r\nEach student carries 6.67 litres.\r\n```\r\n\r\n`round()` drops zeros at the end. So `round(7.5, 2)` shows `7.5`, not `7.50`. That is fine for our programs.\r\n\r\n| You write | Python prints |\r\n| :--- | :--- |\r\n| `f\"{2 + 3}\"` | `5` |\r\n| `f\"Hi {name}\"` with `name = \"Aisha\"` | `Hi Aisha` |\r\n| `f\"{round(10 / 3, 1)}\"` | `3.3` |\r\n| `f\"{{braces}}\"` | `{braces}` |\r\n\r\n## 🧼 Cleaning Text with String Methods\r\n\r\nPeople type messy answers. They add extra spaces or mix capital and small letters. A **method** is a function that belongs to a value. You call it with a dot: `text.strip()`.\r\n\r\n| Method | What it does | `\"  cAIro  \"` becomes |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| `.strip()` | Removes spaces at both ends | `\"cAIro\"` |\r\n| `.lower()` | All small letters | `\"  cairo  \"` |\r\n| `.upper()` | All capital letters | `\"  CAIRO  \"` |\r\n| `.title()` | Capital at the start of each word | `\"  Cairo  \"` |\r\n| `.replace(a, b)` | Swaps text `a` for text `b` | — |\r\n\r\n```python\r\nraw_city = \"   cAIro   \"\r\nclean_city = raw_city.strip().title()\r\nprint(f\"Formatted city: '{clean_city}'\")\r\n\r\nmotto = \"Seek knowledge from the cradle to the grave\"\r\nprint(motto.upper())\r\nprint(motto.replace(\"knowledge\", \"wisdom\"))\r\nprint(motto.startswith(\"Seek\"))\r\nprint(len(motto))\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nFormatted city: 'Cairo'\r\nSEEK KNOWLEDGE FROM THE CRADLE TO THE GRAVE\r\nSeek wisdom from the cradle to the grave\r\nTrue\r\n43\r\n```\r\n\r\n- `raw_city.strip().title()` is **chaining**: first strip, then title-case the result.\r\n- `.startswith()` answers with `True` or `False`.\r\n- `len()` counts the characters, spaces included.\r\n\r\n### Repeating Text\r\n\r\nThe `*` operator repeats a string. It is handy for drawing lines:\r\n\r\n```python\r\nprint(\"=\" * 30)\r\nprint(\"  COMMUNITY CLEAN-UP DAY\")\r\nprint(\"=\" * 30)\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\n==============================\r\n  COMMUNITY CLEAN-UP DAY\r\n==============================\r\n```\r\n\r\n## 🤝 Worked Example: The Sadaqah Pledge Card\r\n\r\nA community centre records voluntary charity (*Sadaqah*) pledges. On a computer, the three values would come from `input()`. Here they are written in the code, with messy spacing on purpose.\r\n\r\n```python\r\ndonor_name = \"  amina yusuf \"      # input(\"Donor name: \")\r\ncause = \"clean water\"              # input(\"Cause: \")\r\namount_text = \"45\"                 # input(\"Pledge amount: \")\r\n\r\ndonor_name = donor_name.strip().title()\r\ncause = cause.strip().title()\r\namount = float(amount_text)\r\nmonthly = amount / 12\r\n\r\nprint(\"-\" * 36)\r\nprint(\"PLEDGE CARD (kept with Amanah)\")\r\nprint(\"-\" * 36)\r\nprint(f\"Donor   : {donor_name}\")\r\nprint(f\"Cause   : {cause}\")\r\nprint(f\"Amount  : {amount}\")\r\nprint(f\"Monthly : {round(monthly, 2)}\")\r\nprint(\"-\" * 36)\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\n------------------------------------\r\nPLEDGE CARD (kept with Amanah)\r\n------------------------------------\r\nDonor   : Amina Yusuf\r\nCause   : Clean Water\r\nAmount  : 45.0\r\nMonthly : 3.75\r\n------------------------------------\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe messy name became `Amina Yusuf`. The text `\"45\"` became a real number, so we could divide it.\r\n\r\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\r\n\r\n**1. Forgetting the `f`.** Without it, Python prints the braces as plain text.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nname = \"Bilal\"\r\nprint(\"Welcome, {name}!\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nWelcome, {name}!\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Fixed version:** `print(f\"Welcome, {name}!\")` prints `Welcome, Bilal!`.\r\n\r\n**2. Doing maths on input without converting it.** Text times a number repeats the text!\r\n\r\n```python\r\ntyped_answer = \"12\"         # input() always gives text\r\nprint(typed_answer * 3)     # wrong: repeats the text\r\nprint(int(typed_answer) * 3)   # right: real maths\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\n121212\r\n36\r\n```\r\n\r\n## ✍️ Try It\r\n\r\nWrite a \"study planner\" card. Store a subject name with messy spaces, such as `\"  physics \"`, and a number of minutes as text, such as `\"45\"`. Clean the subject with `.strip().title()`. Convert the minutes with `int()`. Print: `Today: Physics for 45 minutes (0.75 hours).`\r\n\r\n**Hint:** hours = minutes / 60. Put the calculation inside the braces.\r\n\r\n## 📝 Recap\r\n\r\n- `input()` asks a question and waits. It works on a computer, not in this site's console.\r\n- `input()` always returns a string. Convert with `int()` or `float()` before doing maths.\r\n- An f-string starts with `f` and fills `{ }` gaps with values or small calculations.\r\n- `round(value, places)` keeps decimals short.\r\n- String methods such as `.strip()`, `.title()`, `.lower()` and `.replace()` clean text.\r\n\r\n## 🏆 Practice\r\n\r\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\r\nWhat type of value does `input()` always return? What must you do before adding 10 to it?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\nIt always returns a **string** (`str`). Convert it first with `int()` or `float()`, for example `int(answer) + 10`.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\r\nRewrite this line as an f-string:\r\n\r\n```python\r\nprint(\"The \" + club + \" club has \" + str(members) + \" members.\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```python\r\nprint(f\"The {club} club has {members} members.\")\r\n```\r\n\r\nWith `club = \"Astronomy\"` and `members = 18` it prints: `The Astronomy club has 18 members.`\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\r\nBuild a temperature card for the school weather station.\r\n\r\n1. Store a station name with messy spaces and capitals, for example `\"  north FIELD  \"`.\r\n2. Store a temperature as text, `\"23.4\"` (as if it came from `input()`).\r\n3. Clean the name with `.strip().title()` and convert the temperature with `float()`.\r\n4. Work out Fahrenheit: Celsius × 9 / 5 + 32.\r\n5. Print the station name and both temperatures in one f-string, with Fahrenheit rounded to 1 decimal place.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```python\r\nstation = \"  north FIELD  \"\r\ntemp_text = \"23.4\"\r\n\r\nstation = station.strip().title()\r\ncelsius = float(temp_text)\r\nfahrenheit = celsius * 9 / 5 + 32\r\n\r\nprint(f\"{station}: {celsius} °C = {round(fahrenheit, 1)} °F\")\r\n```\r\n\r\nOutput:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nNorth Field: 23.4 °C = 74.1 °F\r\n```\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n",
  "part-02-control-flow-and-logic/ch04-conditional-branching.md": "# Chapter 4: Conditional Logic & Branching 🚦\r\n\r\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\r\n\r\nEvery morning your sports teacher looks at the sky. If it is raining, PE happens in the hall. Otherwise, the class goes out to the field. The teacher does not need a new plan each day. She has one rule that picks the right path. Programs make choices in exactly this way, using **conditions**: questions whose answer is `True` or `False`.\r\n\r\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\r\n\r\n- Compare values with operators such as `==`, `!=`, `>` and `<=`.\r\n- Choose between paths with `if`, `elif` and `else`.\r\n- Combine conditions with `and`, `or` and `not`.\r\n- Put one decision inside another (nesting).\r\n- Avoid the classic `=` versus `==` mistake.\r\n\r\n## ⚖️ Comparison Operators\r\n\r\nA **comparison** asks a question about two values. The answer is always a Boolean: `True` or `False`.\r\n\r\n| Operator | Meaning | Example | Result |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| `==` | Equal to | `5 == 5` | `True` |\r\n| `!=` | Not equal to | `10 != 2` | `True` |\r\n| `>` | Greater than | `8 > 12` | `False` |\r\n| `<` | Less than | `3 < 7` | `True` |\r\n| `>=` | Greater than or equal to | `50 >= 50` | `True` |\r\n| `<=` | Less than or equal to | `14 <= 10` | `False` |\r\n\r\n```python\r\nbooks_returned = 18\r\ntarget = 20\r\n\r\nprint(books_returned >= target)\r\nprint(books_returned != target)\r\nprint(\"Amina\" == \"amina\")\r\nprint(\"Amina\".lower() == \"amina\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nFalse\r\nTrue\r\nFalse\r\nTrue\r\n```\r\n\r\nString comparisons are **case-sensitive**. `\"Amina\"` and `\"amina\"` are different. Using `.lower()` on both sides is a common fix.\r\n\r\n## 🚦 The `if` Statement\r\n\r\nAn `if` statement runs a block of code **only when its condition is `True`**. Notice the colon `:` at the end of the line, and the 4-space indentation underneath.\r\n\r\n```python\r\ntemperature = 38\r\n\r\nif temperature > 35:\r\n    print(\"Heat warning: drink water and stay in the shade.\")\r\nprint(\"Weather check finished.\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nHeat warning: drink water and stay in the shade.\r\nWeather check finished.\r\n```\r\n\r\nChange `temperature` to `25` and run it again. The warning disappears, but the last line still prints, because it is not indented.\r\n\r\n### Adding `else`\r\n\r\n`else` gives a second path. It runs when the condition is `False`. Exactly one of the two blocks runs.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nis_raining = False\r\n\r\nif is_raining:\r\n    print(\"PE is in the hall today.\")\r\nelse:\r\n    print(\"PE is on the field today.\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nPE is on the field today.\r\n```\r\n\r\n## 🪜 Many Paths with `elif`\r\n\r\n`elif` means \"else if\". It lets you check several conditions in order. Python tests them **from top to bottom** and runs the **first** block whose condition is `True`. Then it skips the rest.\r\n\r\n```\r\n      ┌───────────────┐\r\n      │ score >= 90 ? │\r\n      └──┬─────────┬──┘\r\n     yes │         │ no\r\n         ▼         ▼\r\n  Distinction ┌───────────────┐\r\n              │ score >= 75 ? │\r\n              └──┬─────────┬──┘\r\n             yes │         │ no\r\n                 ▼         ▼\r\n               Merit   ...and so on\r\n```\r\n\r\n```python\r\nscore = 82\r\n\r\nif score >= 90:\r\n    print(\"Distinction - outstanding diligence!\")\r\nelif score >= 75:\r\n    print(\"Merit - good work!\")\r\nelif score >= 50:\r\n    print(\"Pass - keep practising.\")\r\nelse:\r\n    print(\"Needs review - ask your teacher for help.\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nMerit - good work!\r\n```\r\n\r\n`82 >= 90` is `False`, so Python moves on. `82 >= 75` is `True`, so it prints \"Merit\" and stops. It never checks `>= 50`, even though that is also `True`.\r\n\r\nHere is what the same code prints for other scores:\r\n\r\n| `score` | First `True` test | Printed |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| `95` | `score >= 90` | Distinction - outstanding diligence! |\r\n| `82` | `score >= 75` | Merit - good work! |\r\n| `60` | `score >= 50` | Pass - keep practising. |\r\n| `30` | none | Needs review - ask your teacher for help. |\r\n\r\n## 🔗 Combining Conditions: `and`, `or`, `not`\r\n\r\nReal decisions often depend on more than one fact. Python has three **logical operators**:\r\n\r\n- `and` is `True` only if **both** sides are `True`.\r\n- `or` is `True` if **at least one** side is `True`.\r\n- `not` flips a value: `not True` is `False`.\r\n\r\n| A | B | `A and B` | `A or B` |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| `True` | `True` | `True` | `True` |\r\n| `True` | `False` | `False` | `True` |\r\n| `False` | `True` | `False` | `True` |\r\n| `False` | `False` | `False` | `False` |\r\n\r\n```python\r\nfinished_python_course = True\r\nattendance = 92\r\nhas_warning = False\r\n\r\nif finished_python_course and attendance >= 85 and not has_warning:\r\n    print(\"Welcome to the Robotics Lab!\")\r\nelse:\r\n    print(\"Please complete the requirements first.\")\r\n\r\nis_weekend = True\r\nis_holiday = False\r\nif is_weekend or is_holiday:\r\n    print(\"The library opens at 10:00.\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nWelcome to the Robotics Lab!\r\nThe library opens at 10:00.\r\n```\r\n\r\n## 🪆 Nested Decisions\r\n\r\nYou can put an `if` inside another `if`. This is called **nesting**. The inner block is indented one more level (8 spaces).\r\n\r\n```python\r\nhas_ticket = True\r\ngroup_size = 34\r\n\r\nif has_ticket:\r\n    print(\"Welcome to the planetarium!\")\r\n    if group_size > 30:\r\n        print(\"Large group: please use the side entrance.\")\r\n    else:\r\n        print(\"Please use the main entrance.\")\r\nelse:\r\n    print(\"Please collect your tickets at the desk.\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nWelcome to the planetarium!\r\nLarge group: please use the side entrance.\r\n```\r\n\r\nKeep nesting shallow. Two levels is usually enough. If you need more, try `and` or `elif` instead.\r\n\r\n## 🌱 Worked Example: Plant Irrigation Advisor\r\n\r\nA school garden has a soil sensor. The program decides whether to switch on the drip watering system. On a computer, the three values could come from `input()`. Here they are written in the code.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nsoil_moisture = 24        # percent, 0 to 100\r\nrain_expected = False\r\ntemperature = 37.0        # degrees Celsius\r\n\r\nprint(\"--- GARDEN SYSTEM CHECK ---\")\r\n\r\nif soil_moisture < 30 and not rain_expected:\r\n    print(\"Status: watering ON\")\r\n    if temperature > 35:\r\n        print(\"Note: very hot day, watering time increased.\")\r\nelif soil_moisture < 30 and rain_expected:\r\n    print(\"Status: waiting - rain is expected soon.\")\r\nelif soil_moisture >= 70:\r\n    print(\"Status: soil is wet enough - saving water.\")\r\nelse:\r\n    print(\"Status: moisture is fine - keep monitoring.\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\n--- GARDEN SYSTEM CHECK ---\r\nStatus: watering ON\r\nNote: very hot day, watering time increased.\r\n```\r\n\r\nTry changing `rain_expected` to `True`. Then try `soil_moisture = 75`. Predict each result before you run it. Saving water is a form of care for the earth (*Iḥsān*).\r\n\r\n## 🔮 Predict the Output\r\n\r\n```python\r\nage = 13\r\nhas_permission_slip = False\r\n\r\nif age >= 12 and has_permission_slip:\r\n    print(\"Join the trip\")\r\nelif age >= 12:\r\n    print(\"Bring your slip\")\r\nelse:\r\n    print(\"Too young for this trip\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```text\r\nBring your slip\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe first test needs both parts `True`, but the slip is `False`. The second test, `age >= 12`, is `True`.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\r\n\r\n**1. Using `=` instead of `==`.** A single `=` stores a value. It cannot ask a question.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nanswer = \"yes\"\r\nif answer = \"yes\":\r\n    print(\"Thank you!\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nSyntaxError: invalid syntax. Maybe you meant '==' or ':=' instead of '='?\r\n```\r\n\r\nPython even guesses what you meant. (You can ignore `:=`; it is an advanced operator.)\r\n\r\n**Fixed version:** `if answer == \"yes\":` (two equals signs to compare).\r\n\r\n**2. Putting the `elif` tests in the wrong order.** Python stops at the first `True` test.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nscore = 95\r\nif score >= 50:\r\n    print(\"Pass\")\r\nelif score >= 90:\r\n    print(\"Distinction\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nPass\r\n```\r\n\r\nA 95 should be a Distinction! **Fix:** test the biggest number first (`>= 90`, then `>= 50`).\r\n\r\n## ✍️ Try It\r\n\r\nA library charges no fines, but it sends reminders. Store `days_late = 9`. Print:\r\n\r\n- `\"On time - thank you!\"` if `days_late` is 0,\r\n- `\"Friendly reminder sent\"` if it is 1 to 7,\r\n- `\"Please visit the desk\"` if it is more than 7.\r\n\r\n**Hint:** use `if`, `elif` and `else`. Test `== 0` first, then `<= 7`.\r\n\r\n## 📝 Recap\r\n\r\n- Comparisons (`==`, `!=`, `>`, `<`, `>=`, `<=`) give `True` or `False`.\r\n- `if` runs a block only when its condition is `True`. End the line with `:` and indent the block.\r\n- `elif` checks more conditions in order; the first `True` one wins. `else` catches everything left.\r\n- `and`, `or` and `not` combine or flip conditions.\r\n- `=` stores a value; `==` compares two values.\r\n\r\n## 🏆 Practice\r\n\r\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\r\nWhat does each expression give? `7 != 7`, `10 >= 10`, `True and False`, `not False`\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n`False`, `True`, `False`, `True`.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\r\nWrite code that stores `litres_used = 140` and prints `\"Water use is low\"` if it is under 100, `\"Water use is normal\"` if it is 100 to 150, and `\"Water use is high\"` otherwise.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```python\r\nlitres_used = 140\r\n\r\nif litres_used < 100:\r\n    print(\"Water use is low\")\r\nelif litres_used <= 150:\r\n    print(\"Water use is normal\")\r\nelse:\r\n    print(\"Water use is high\")\r\n```\r\n\r\nOutput:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nWater use is normal\r\n```\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\r\nBuild a \"field trip check\" for a school bus.\r\n\r\n1. Store `students = 38`, `seats = 40` and `driver_ready = True`.\r\n2. If the driver is not ready, print `\"Wait for the driver\"`.\r\n3. Otherwise, if there are more students than seats, print how many must take the second bus.\r\n4. Otherwise, print `\"Everyone fits - let's go!\"` and how many seats are empty.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```python\r\nstudents = 38\r\nseats = 40\r\ndriver_ready = True\r\n\r\nif not driver_ready:\r\n    print(\"Wait for the driver\")\r\nelif students > seats:\r\n    print(f\"{students - seats} students take the second bus\")\r\nelse:\r\n    print(\"Everyone fits - let's go!\")\r\n    print(f\"Empty seats: {seats - students}\")\r\n```\r\n\r\nOutput:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nEveryone fits - let's go!\r\nEmpty seats: 2\r\n```\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n",
  "part-02-control-flow-and-logic/ch05-loops-and-sequences.md": "# Chapter 5: Loops & Sequences (`for` & `while`) 🔁\r\n\r\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\r\n\r\nAt an organic farm, crates of oranges roll past on a conveyor belt. A worker checks each crate, stamps it and waits for the next one. When the last crate has passed, the job is done. Nobody writes a new instruction for every crate. There is one instruction: \"for every crate, check and stamp\". In Python, that kind of repeated instruction is called a **loop**.\r\n\r\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\r\n\r\n- Repeat code a set number of times with `for` and `range()`.\r\n- Loop over each character of a string.\r\n- Keep running totals and counts inside a loop.\r\n- Repeat code until a condition changes with `while`.\r\n- Control loops with `break` and `continue`, and avoid infinite loops.\r\n\r\n## 🔂 The `for` Loop and `range()`\r\n\r\nA `for` loop repeats a block once for each value in a **sequence** (an ordered row of values). `range(5)` makes the sequence `0, 1, 2, 3, 4`.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nfor day in range(5):\r\n    print(f\"Day {day}: read one page of the science book\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nDay 0: read one page of the science book\r\nDay 1: read one page of the science book\r\nDay 2: read one page of the science book\r\nDay 3: read one page of the science book\r\nDay 4: read one page of the science book\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe variable `day` is the **loop variable**. It takes the next value on each pass. Each pass through the loop is called an **iteration**.\r\n\r\n`range(5)` starts at `0` and stops **before** `5`. It gives five numbers, but `5` itself is not one of them.\r\n\r\n### Choosing Start, Stop and Step\r\n\r\n`range()` can take up to three numbers: `range(start, stop, step)`.\r\n\r\n| Code | Numbers produced |\r\n| :--- | :--- |\r\n| `range(4)` | 0, 1, 2, 3 |\r\n| `range(1, 6)` | 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 |\r\n| `range(10, 51, 10)` | 10, 20, 30, 40, 50 |\r\n| `range(5, 0, -1)` | 5, 4, 3, 2, 1 |\r\n\r\n```python\r\nfor percent in range(10, 51, 10):\r\n    print(f\"Upload {percent}% complete\")\r\n\r\nfor count in range(3, 0, -1):\r\n    print(count)\r\nprint(\"Launch the weather balloon!\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nUpload 10% complete\r\nUpload 20% complete\r\nUpload 30% complete\r\nUpload 40% complete\r\nUpload 50% complete\r\n3\r\n2\r\n1\r\nLaunch the weather balloon!\r\n```\r\n\r\nA negative step counts down. The stop value is still left out, so `range(3, 0, -1)` ends at `1`.\r\n\r\n## 🔤 Looping Over a String\r\n\r\nA string is also a sequence: a row of characters. A `for` loop can visit each character in turn.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nword = \"SALAM\"\r\nfor letter in word:\r\n    print(letter)\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nS\r\nA\r\nL\r\nA\r\nM\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis lets us count things inside text. Here we count the vowels in a sentence:\r\n\r\n```python\r\nsentence = \"Knowledge is light\"\r\nvowels = 0\r\n\r\nfor letter in sentence.lower():\r\n    if letter in \"aeiou\":\r\n        vowels += 1\r\n\r\nprint(f\"Vowels found: {vowels}\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nVowels found: 5\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe keyword `in` checks whether one piece of text appears inside another. `\"e\" in \"aeiou\"` is `True`.\r\n\r\n## ➕ Running Totals and Counters\r\n\r\nTwo loop patterns appear again and again:\r\n\r\n- An **accumulator** is a variable that collects a total. Start it at `0` before the loop, then add to it inside.\r\n- A **counter** counts how many times something happened. It also starts at `0`.\r\n\r\n```python\r\ntotal_pages = 0\r\nlong_days = 0\r\n\r\nfor day in range(1, 8):\r\n    pages = day * 5          # day 1 = 5 pages, day 2 = 10 pages...\r\n    total_pages += pages\r\n    if pages > 20:\r\n        long_days += 1\r\n    print(f\"Day {day}: {pages} pages (total so far {total_pages})\")\r\n\r\nprint(f\"Week total: {total_pages} pages\")\r\nprint(f\"Days with more than 20 pages: {long_days}\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nDay 1: 5 pages (total so far 5)\r\nDay 2: 10 pages (total so far 15)\r\nDay 3: 15 pages (total so far 30)\r\nDay 4: 20 pages (total so far 50)\r\nDay 5: 25 pages (total so far 75)\r\nDay 6: 30 pages (total so far 105)\r\nDay 7: 35 pages (total so far 140)\r\nWeek total: 140 pages\r\nDays with more than 20 pages: 3\r\n```\r\n\r\nWatch how `total_pages` grows on every line. Days 5, 6 and 7 had more than 20 pages, so `long_days` is 3.\r\n\r\n## ⏳ The `while` Loop\r\n\r\nA `while` loop repeats **as long as its condition is `True`**. Use it when you do not know in advance how many passes you need.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nbattery = 100\r\n\r\nwhile battery > 20:\r\n    print(f\"Rover exploring. Battery: {battery}%\")\r\n    battery -= 25\r\n\r\nprint(f\"Battery at {battery}%. Returning to the solar charger.\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nRover exploring. Battery: 100%\r\nRover exploring. Battery: 75%\r\nRover exploring. Battery: 50%\r\nRover exploring. Battery: 25%\r\nBattery at 0%. Returning to the solar charger.\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe steps are: check the condition, run the block, check again. After four patrols the battery is `0`, `0 > 20` is `False`, and the loop ends.\r\n\r\n### Example: Saving Up for a Donation\r\n\r\nYusuf puts 15 coins a week into a charity box. How many weeks until he reaches 100 coins?\r\n\r\n```python\r\ncoins = 0\r\nweeks = 0\r\n\r\nwhile coins < 100:\r\n    coins += 15\r\n    weeks += 1\r\n\r\nprint(f\"After {weeks} weeks Yusuf has {coins} coins to donate.\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nAfter 7 weeks Yusuf has 105 coins to donate.\r\n```\r\n\r\n| | `for` loop | `while` loop |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| Use when | You know how many times, or have a sequence | You repeat until something changes |\r\n| Example | Each day of the week | Until the battery is low |\r\n| Danger | Few | Forgetting to change the condition |\r\n\r\n## 🛑 Controlling Loops: `break` and `continue`\r\n\r\n- `break` stops the loop **immediately**. Python jumps to the first line after the loop.\r\n- `continue` skips the rest of **this** pass and jumps to the next one.\r\n\r\n```python\r\ntotal = 0\r\n\r\nfor day in range(1, 11):\r\n    if day % 7 == 0:\r\n        print(f\"Day {day}: rest day, no collection\")\r\n        continue\r\n    total += 30\r\n    print(f\"Day {day}: collected 30 bottles, total {total}\")\r\n    if total >= 200:\r\n        print(\"Target of 200 bottles reached!\")\r\n        break\r\n\r\nprint(\"Recycling drive finished.\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nDay 1: collected 30 bottles, total 30\r\nDay 2: collected 30 bottles, total 60\r\nDay 3: collected 30 bottles, total 90\r\nDay 4: collected 30 bottles, total 120\r\nDay 5: collected 30 bottles, total 150\r\nDay 6: collected 30 bottles, total 180\r\nDay 7: rest day, no collection\r\nDay 8: collected 30 bottles, total 210\r\nTarget of 200 bottles reached!\r\nRecycling drive finished.\r\n```\r\n\r\nDay 7 is skipped by `continue`. On day 8 the total reaches 210, so `break` ends the loop early. Days 9 and 10 never run.\r\n\r\n## ☀️ Worked Example: Solar Energy Over a Day\r\n\r\nA school's solar panels make different amounts of energy at different hours. This loop runs from 6:00 to 18:00 and adds up the energy.\r\n\r\n```python\r\ntotal_kwh = 0\r\n\r\nfor hour in range(6, 19):\r\n    if 11 <= hour <= 15:\r\n        energy = 4.5\r\n        light = \"strong sun\"\r\n    elif 9 <= hour <= 17:\r\n        energy = 2.8\r\n        light = \"some sun\"\r\n    else:\r\n        energy = 0.9\r\n        light = \"low light\"\r\n    total_kwh += energy\r\n    print(f\"{hour}:00  {light}  +{energy} kWh\")\r\n\r\nprint(f\"Total clean energy today: {round(total_kwh, 1)} kWh\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\n6:00  low light  +0.9 kWh\r\n7:00  low light  +0.9 kWh\r\n8:00  low light  +0.9 kWh\r\n9:00  some sun  +2.8 kWh\r\n10:00  some sun  +2.8 kWh\r\n11:00  strong sun  +4.5 kWh\r\n12:00  strong sun  +4.5 kWh\r\n13:00  strong sun  +4.5 kWh\r\n14:00  strong sun  +4.5 kWh\r\n15:00  strong sun  +4.5 kWh\r\n16:00  some sun  +2.8 kWh\r\n17:00  some sun  +2.8 kWh\r\n18:00  low light  +0.9 kWh\r\nTotal clean energy today: 37.3 kWh\r\n```\r\n\r\n`11 <= hour <= 15` is a neat Python shortcut. It means `hour >= 11 and hour <= 15`. We use `round()` at the end because adding decimals can leave tiny leftover digits.\r\n\r\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\r\n\r\n**1. A `while` loop that never ends.** If the condition never becomes `False`, the loop runs forever. This is an **infinite loop**.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nwater_level = 10\r\nwhile water_level > 0:\r\n    print(\"Pumping water...\")\r\n    # forgot: water_level -= 1\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis prints \"Pumping water...\" again and again. Stop it with <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>C</kbd> (on a computer) or the Stop button. **Fix:** make sure the loop changes the variable in its condition, for example `water_level -= 1`.\r\n\r\n**2. Expecting `range()` to include the stop number.**\r\n\r\n```python\r\nfor n in range(1, 5):\r\n    print(n)\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\n1\r\n2\r\n3\r\n4\r\n```\r\n\r\nThere is no `5`. **Fix:** to count 1 to 5, write `range(1, 6)`.\r\n\r\n## ✍️ Try It\r\n\r\nPrint the 7 times table from 7 × 1 to 7 × 10, one line each, like `7 x 3 = 21`. Then print the sum of all ten answers.\r\n\r\n**Hint:** use `for n in range(1, 11):` and an accumulator that starts at `0`. The sum should be 385.\r\n\r\n## 📝 Recap\r\n\r\n- A `for` loop runs once for each value in a sequence, such as `range()` or a string.\r\n- `range(start, stop, step)` stops **before** the stop value.\r\n- Accumulators collect totals; counters count events. Both start at `0` before the loop.\r\n- A `while` loop repeats while its condition is `True`. Always change the condition inside the loop.\r\n- `break` leaves a loop early; `continue` skips to the next pass.\r\n\r\n## 🏆 Practice\r\n\r\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\r\nWhich numbers does `range(2, 12, 3)` produce?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n`2, 5, 8, 11`. It starts at 2, adds 3 each time, and stops before 12.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\r\nA plant is 4 cm tall and grows 3 cm every week. Use a `while` loop to find how many weeks until it is at least 30 cm tall. Print the weeks and the final height.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```python\r\nheight = 4\r\nweeks = 0\r\n\r\nwhile height < 30:\r\n    height += 3\r\n    weeks += 1\r\n\r\nprint(f\"{weeks} weeks, height {height} cm\")\r\n```\r\n\r\nOutput:\r\n\r\n```text\r\n9 weeks, height 31 cm\r\n```\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\r\nBuild a \"quiet hours\" checker for a study hall that is open from 8:00 to 20:00.\r\n\r\n1. Loop over the hours 8 to 20 (including 20).\r\n2. Skip 13:00 with `continue` and print `\"13:00 lunch break\"`.\r\n3. For hours before 12, print `\"quiet study\"`. For other hours, print `\"group study\"`.\r\n4. Count how many group study hours there are and print the count at the end.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```python\r\ngroup_hours = 0\r\n\r\nfor hour in range(8, 21):\r\n    if hour == 13:\r\n        print(\"13:00 lunch break\")\r\n        continue\r\n    if hour < 12:\r\n        print(f\"{hour}:00 quiet study\")\r\n    else:\r\n        print(f\"{hour}:00 group study\")\r\n        group_hours += 1\r\n\r\nprint(f\"Group study hours: {group_hours}\")\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe last line prints `Group study hours: 8` (12:00 and 14:00 to 20:00).\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n",
  "part-03-data-structures-and-functions/ch06-lists-and-tuples.md": "# Chapter 6: Lists & Tuples 📋\r\n\r\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\r\n\r\nYour class teacher keeps one attendance register, not thirty separate notes. Each student has a numbered line. She can read line 5, add a new student at the bottom, or count the lines. A Python **list** is that register. It keeps many values together, in order, under one name.\r\n\r\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\r\n\r\n- Create lists and read items by their index (position number).\r\n- Add, change, remove and sort items with list methods.\r\n- Loop over a list to total, count and filter values.\r\n- Take a part of a list with slicing.\r\n- Use tuples for data that should never change.\r\n\r\n## 🚂 Creating a List\r\n\r\nA **list** is written with square brackets `[ ]`. Items are separated by commas. Think of a train: each wagon holds one item, and the wagons are numbered **starting at 0**. The position number is called the **index**.\r\n\r\n```\r\nIndex:   0          1           2\r\n      [\"Algebra\", \"Astronomy\", \"Ethics\"]\r\nBack:   -3         -2          -1\r\n```\r\n\r\n```python\r\nsubjects = [\"Algebra\", \"Astronomy\", \"Ethics\", \"Medicine\"]\r\n\r\nprint(subjects)\r\nprint(subjects[0])\r\nprint(subjects[2])\r\nprint(subjects[-1])\r\nprint(len(subjects))\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\n['Algebra', 'Astronomy', 'Ethics', 'Medicine']\r\nAlgebra\r\nEthics\r\nMedicine\r\n4\r\n```\r\n\r\n- `subjects[0]` is the **first** item, because counting starts at 0.\r\n- A **negative index** counts from the end. `-1` is the last item.\r\n- `len()` gives the number of items.\r\n\r\nA list can hold any type: numbers, strings, Booleans, or a mix. It can even be empty: `[]`.\r\n\r\n### Changing an Item\r\n\r\nLists are **mutable**: you can change them after creating them. Put a new value at an index with `=`.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nreadings = [22, 24, 99, 23]\r\nreadings[2] = 25      # fix a faulty sensor reading\r\nprint(readings)\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\n[22, 24, 25, 23]\r\n```\r\n\r\n## 🛠️ List Methods\r\n\r\nA list comes with built-in methods. Here are the most useful ones:\r\n\r\n| Method | What it does |\r\n| :--- | :--- |\r\n| `.append(x)` | Adds `x` at the end |\r\n| `.insert(i, x)` | Puts `x` at index `i` and moves the rest along |\r\n| `.remove(x)` | Removes the first item equal to `x` |\r\n| `.pop(i)` | Removes the item at index `i` and gives it back |\r\n| `.sort()` | Sorts the list in place, smallest first |\r\n\r\n```python\r\nbooks = [\"Clean Code\", \"Linear Algebra\"]\r\n\r\nbooks.append(\"Python for Science\")\r\nprint(books)\r\n\r\nbooks.insert(1, \"Algorithmic Thinking\")\r\nprint(books)\r\n\r\nbooks.remove(\"Clean Code\")\r\nprint(books)\r\n\r\nfinished = books.pop(0)\r\nprint(\"Finished reading:\", finished)\r\nprint(books)\r\n\r\nscores = [42, 12, 88, 3, 27]\r\nscores.sort()\r\nprint(scores)\r\nscores.sort(reverse=True)\r\nprint(scores)\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\n['Clean Code', 'Linear Algebra', 'Python for Science']\r\n['Clean Code', 'Algorithmic Thinking', 'Linear Algebra', 'Python for Science']\r\n['Algorithmic Thinking', 'Linear Algebra', 'Python for Science']\r\nFinished reading: Algorithmic Thinking\r\n['Linear Algebra', 'Python for Science']\r\n[3, 12, 27, 42, 88]\r\n[88, 42, 27, 12, 3]\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Is It in the List?\r\n\r\nThe keyword `in` checks whether a value is in a list. It gives `True` or `False`. `not in` asks the opposite question.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nvolunteers = [\"Amina\", \"Bilal\", \"Hana\"]\r\nprint(\"Hana\" in volunteers)\r\nprint(\"Omar\" in volunteers)\r\n\r\nif \"Omar\" not in volunteers:\r\n    volunteers.append(\"Omar\")\r\nprint(volunteers)\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nTrue\r\nFalse\r\n['Amina', 'Bilal', 'Hana', 'Omar']\r\n```\r\n\r\n## 🔁 Looping Over a List\r\n\r\nA `for` loop can visit every item of a list, one by one, just as it visited each letter of a string.\r\n\r\n```python\r\ndonations = [25, 40, 15, 60]\r\ntotal = 0\r\n\r\nfor amount in donations:\r\n    total += amount\r\n    print(f\"Added {amount}, running total {total}\")\r\n\r\nprint(f\"Total raised: {total}\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nAdded 25, running total 25\r\nAdded 40, running total 65\r\nAdded 15, running total 80\r\nAdded 60, running total 140\r\nTotal raised: 140\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Built-in Helpers: `sum`, `max`, `min`\r\n\r\nPython can do common jobs for you. These work on lists of numbers:\r\n\r\n```python\r\ntemperatures = [22.1, 23.5, 24.0, 25.2, 26.8]\r\n\r\nprint(sum(temperatures))\r\nprint(max(temperatures))\r\nprint(min(temperatures))\r\naverage = sum(temperatures) / len(temperatures)\r\nprint(f\"Average: {round(average, 1)}\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\n121.6\r\n26.8\r\n22.1\r\nAverage: 24.3\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe average is `24.32`, so `round(average, 1)` shows `24.3`. Rounding matters with decimals. Try `print(0.1 + 0.2)`: it shows `0.30000000000000004`, because computers store decimals in binary. `round()` tidies such leftovers.\r\n\r\n### Building a New List in a Loop\r\n\r\nTo **filter** a list (keep only some items), start with an empty list and `append` the items you want.\r\n\r\n```python\r\ngrades = [88, 92, 79, 95, 84, 91]\r\nhonour_roll = []\r\n\r\nfor grade in grades:\r\n    if grade >= 90:\r\n        honour_roll.append(grade)\r\n\r\nprint(honour_roll)\r\nprint(f\"{len(honour_roll)} students made the honour roll\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\n[92, 95, 91]\r\n3 students made the honour roll\r\n```\r\n\r\n## ✂️ Slicing: Taking Part of a List\r\n\r\nA **slice** copies a part of a list. Write `[start:stop]`. Like `range()`, it stops **before** `stop`. The original list does not change.\r\n\r\n| Slice | Meaning |\r\n| :--- | :--- |\r\n| `days[0:3]` | Items at index 0, 1, 2 |\r\n| `days[:2]` | From the start up to index 2 |\r\n| `days[3:]` | From index 3 to the end |\r\n| `days[-2:]` | The last two items |\r\n\r\n```python\r\ndays = [\"Sat\", \"Sun\", \"Mon\", \"Tue\", \"Wed\", \"Thu\", \"Fri\"]\r\n\r\nprint(days[0:3])\r\nprint(days[3:])\r\nprint(days[-2:])\r\nprint(days)\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\n['Sat', 'Sun', 'Mon']\r\n['Tue', 'Wed', 'Thu', 'Fri']\r\n['Thu', 'Fri']\r\n['Sat', 'Sun', 'Mon', 'Tue', 'Wed', 'Thu', 'Fri']\r\n```\r\n\r\n## 🔒 Tuples: Lists That Cannot Change\r\n\r\nA **tuple** looks like a list, but uses round brackets `( )`. A tuple is **immutable**: once made, it cannot be changed. Use it for values that belong together and must stay fixed, like map coordinates or a date.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nrelief_hub = (30.0444, 31.2357)     # latitude, longitude\r\nprint(relief_hub[0])\r\nprint(len(relief_hub))\r\n\r\nlatitude, longitude = relief_hub     # unpacking\r\nprint(f\"Latitude {latitude}, longitude {longitude}\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\n30.0444\r\n2\r\nLatitude 30.0444, longitude 31.2357\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Unpacking** puts each item of a tuple into its own variable, in order. Trying to change a tuple causes an error:\r\n\r\n```python\r\nrelief_hub = (30.0444, 31.2357)\r\nrelief_hub[0] = 31.0\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nTypeError: 'tuple' object does not support item assignment\r\n```\r\n\r\n| | List | Tuple |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| Brackets | `[1, 2, 3]` | `(1, 2, 3)` |\r\n| Can change? | Yes (mutable) | No (immutable) |\r\n| Good for | A register that grows | Fixed facts: coordinates, dates |\r\n\r\nUsing a tuple protects data that must not be changed by accident. That is a small act of trust (*Amānah*).\r\n\r\n## 📊 Worked Example: Class Results Summary\r\n\r\n```python\r\ngrades = [88, 92, 79, 95, 84, 91, 68, 89]\r\n\r\naverage = sum(grades) / len(grades)\r\nhonour_roll = []\r\nneeds_help = 0\r\n\r\nfor grade in grades:\r\n    if grade >= 90:\r\n        honour_roll.append(grade)\r\n    if grade < 70:\r\n        needs_help += 1\r\n\r\nprint(\"CLASS RESULTS SUMMARY\")\r\nprint(f\"Exams marked : {len(grades)}\")\r\nprint(f\"Average      : {round(average, 1)}\")\r\nprint(f\"Highest      : {max(grades)}\")\r\nprint(f\"Lowest       : {min(grades)}\")\r\nprint(f\"Honour roll  : {honour_roll}\")\r\nprint(f\"Extra support: {needs_help} student(s)\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nCLASS RESULTS SUMMARY\r\nExams marked : 8\r\nAverage      : 85.8\r\nHighest      : 95\r\nLowest       : 68\r\nHonour roll  : [92, 95, 91]\r\nExtra support: 1 student(s)\r\n```\r\n\r\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\r\n\r\n**1. Using an index that does not exist.** A list of 3 items has indexes 0, 1 and 2. There is no index 3.\r\n\r\n```python\r\ncolours = [\"red\", \"green\", \"blue\"]\r\nprint(colours[3])\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nIndexError: list index out of range\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Fix:** the last index is always `len(list) - 1`, or simply use `colours[-1]`.\r\n\r\n**2. Storing the result of `.sort()`.** `.sort()` changes the list itself and gives back nothing (`None`).\r\n\r\n```python\r\nscores = [30, 10, 20]\r\nsorted_scores = scores.sort()\r\nprint(sorted_scores)\r\nprint(scores)\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nNone\r\n[10, 20, 30]\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Fix:** call `scores.sort()` on its own line, then use `scores`.\r\n\r\n## ✍️ Try It\r\n\r\nMake a list of the minutes you spent reading each day this week, for example `[20, 35, 0, 15, 40, 25, 30]`. Print the total, the longest day, and how many days had **no** reading.\r\n\r\n**Hint:** use `sum()` and `max()`, then a loop with a counter for the zeros.\r\n\r\n## 📝 Recap\r\n\r\n- A list keeps many values in order: `[a, b, c]`. Indexes start at 0; `-1` is the last item.\r\n- Lists are mutable: `.append()`, `.insert()`, `.remove()`, `.pop()` and `.sort()` change them.\r\n- Loop over a list with `for`. `sum()`, `max()`, `min()` and `len()` summarise numbers.\r\n- To filter, start with `[]` and `append` the items you want.\r\n- Slices like `[1:4]` copy part of a list. Tuples `( )` are like lists that cannot change.\r\n\r\n## 🏆 Practice\r\n\r\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\r\nGiven `planets = [\"Mercury\", \"Venus\", \"Earth\", \"Mars\"]`, what is `planets[1]`, `planets[-1]` and `planets[1:3]`?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n`\"Venus\"`, `\"Mars\"` and `[\"Venus\", \"Earth\"]`.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\r\nStart with `queue = [\"Amina\", \"Bilal\"]`. Add `\"Hana\"` at the end, put `\"Omar\"` at the front, then remove the first person with `.pop(0)` and print who was served and the queue that is left.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```python\r\nqueue = [\"Amina\", \"Bilal\"]\r\nqueue.append(\"Hana\")\r\nqueue.insert(0, \"Omar\")\r\nserved = queue.pop(0)\r\nprint(\"Served:\", served)\r\nprint(queue)\r\n```\r\n\r\nOutput:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nServed: Omar\r\n['Amina', 'Bilal', 'Hana']\r\n```\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\r\nA clean-up team weighed the rubbish they collected each day (kg): `[12.5, 8.0, 15.5, 4.0, 10.0]`.\r\n\r\n1. Print the total and the average (rounded to 1 decimal place).\r\n2. Build a new list of the \"big days\" (more than 10 kg) with a loop and `append`.\r\n3. Print the big days sorted from largest to smallest.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```python\r\nweights = [12.5, 8.0, 15.5, 4.0, 10.0]\r\n\r\ntotal = sum(weights)\r\naverage = total / len(weights)\r\nprint(f\"Total: {total} kg, average: {round(average, 1)} kg\")\r\n\r\nbig_days = []\r\nfor kg in weights:\r\n    if kg > 10:\r\n        big_days.append(kg)\r\n\r\nbig_days.sort(reverse=True)\r\nprint(\"Big days:\", big_days)\r\n```\r\n\r\nOutput:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nTotal: 50.0 kg, average: 10.0 kg\r\nBig days: [15.5, 12.5]\r\n```\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n",
  "part-03-data-structures-and-functions/ch07-dictionaries-and-sets.md": "# Chapter 7: Dictionaries & Sets 📖\r\n\r\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\r\n\r\nIn the school library, every book has a catalogue card. The card does not say \"item 0, item 1, item 2\". It says \"Title: …\", \"Author: …\", \"Year: …\". You find what you need by reading the **label**, not by counting positions. A Python **dictionary** works like that card. A **set** is different: it is like a box of name badges where no name can appear twice.\r\n\r\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\r\n\r\n- Store labelled data in a dictionary as key-value pairs.\r\n- Read, add, change and remove entries, and use `.get()` to read safely.\r\n- Loop over a dictionary and count things with it.\r\n- Keep a list of dictionaries as a small table of records.\r\n- Remove duplicates and compare groups with sets.\r\n\r\n## 🗂️ Creating a Dictionary\r\n\r\nA **dictionary** stores **key-value pairs**. The **key** is the label. The **value** is the information. Write a dictionary with curly braces `{ }`, a colon between each key and value, and commas between pairs.\r\n\r\n```\r\n key          value\r\n\"title\"   →  \"Book of Optics\"\r\n\"author\"  →  \"Ibn al-Haytham\"\r\n\"year\"    →  1021\r\n```\r\n\r\n```python\r\nbook = {\r\n    \"title\": \"Book of Optics\",\r\n    \"author\": \"Ibn al-Haytham\",\r\n    \"year\": 1021,\r\n    \"is_available\": True\r\n}\r\n\r\nprint(book[\"title\"])\r\nprint(book[\"year\"])\r\nprint(len(book))\r\nprint(book)\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nBook of Optics\r\n1021\r\n4\r\n{'title': 'Book of Optics', 'author': 'Ibn al-Haytham', 'year': 1021, 'is_available': True}\r\n```\r\n\r\nYou read a value with square brackets and the **key**: `book[\"title\"]`. Keys are usually strings. Each key appears only once in a dictionary.\r\n\r\n## ✏️ Adding, Changing and Removing\r\n\r\n```python\r\nbook = {\"title\": \"Book of Optics\", \"year\": 1021, \"is_available\": True}\r\n\r\nbook[\"category\"] = \"Physics\"     # new key: adds a pair\r\nbook[\"is_available\"] = False     # existing key: changes the value\r\nprint(book)\r\n\r\nremoved = book.pop(\"year\")       # removes the pair, gives back the value\r\nprint(\"Removed:\", removed)\r\nprint(book)\r\n\r\nprint(\"title\" in book)\r\nprint(\"year\" in book)\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\n{'title': 'Book of Optics', 'year': 1021, 'is_available': False, 'category': 'Physics'}\r\nRemoved: 1021\r\n{'title': 'Book of Optics', 'is_available': False, 'category': 'Physics'}\r\nTrue\r\nFalse\r\n```\r\n\r\n- Assigning to a **new** key adds a pair. Assigning to an **existing** key replaces its value.\r\n- `.pop(key)` removes a pair and returns its value.\r\n- `in` checks whether a **key** exists.\r\n\r\n### Reading Safely with `.get()`\r\n\r\nAsking for a key that does not exist causes an error (see Common Mistake). `.get(key, default)` is safer. If the key is missing, it gives back the default value instead.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nbook = {\"title\": \"Book of Optics\", \"author\": \"Ibn al-Haytham\"}\r\n\r\nprint(book.get(\"author\", \"Unknown\"))\r\nprint(book.get(\"publisher\", \"Unknown\"))\r\nprint(book.get(\"publisher\"))\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nIbn al-Haytham\r\nUnknown\r\nNone\r\n```\r\n\r\nWith no default, `.get()` returns `None`, Python's word for \"nothing here\".\r\n\r\n## 🔁 Looping Over a Dictionary\r\n\r\nA `for` loop over a dictionary visits its **keys**. Use `.items()` to get each key **and** its value together.\r\n\r\n| Code | Gives you |\r\n| :--- | :--- |\r\n| `for k in d:` | each key |\r\n| `for v in d.values():` | each value |\r\n| `for k, v in d.items():` | each key and value |\r\n\r\n```python\r\nstock = {\"notebooks\": 40, \"pencils\": 120, \"rulers\": 15}\r\n\r\nfor item in stock:\r\n    print(item)\r\n\r\nfor item, count in stock.items():\r\n    print(f\"{item}: {count}\")\r\n\r\nprint(\"Total items:\", sum(stock.values()))\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nnotebooks\r\npencils\r\nrulers\r\nnotebooks: 40\r\npencils: 120\r\nrulers: 15\r\nTotal items: 175\r\n```\r\n\r\nDictionaries remember the order in which you added the keys, so loops follow that order.\r\n\r\n## 🧮 Counting with a Dictionary\r\n\r\nA very common job is counting how often each thing appears. Start with an empty dictionary `{}`. For each item, add 1 to its count. `.get(item, 0)` gives 0 the first time an item is seen.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nvotes = [\"tree planting\", \"book drive\", \"tree planting\",\r\n         \"beach clean-up\", \"tree planting\", \"book drive\"]\r\n\r\ntally = {}\r\nfor choice in votes:\r\n    tally[choice] = tally.get(choice, 0) + 1\r\n\r\nprint(tally)\r\nfor choice, count in tally.items():\r\n    print(f\"{choice}: {count} vote(s)\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\n{'tree planting': 3, 'book drive': 2, 'beach clean-up': 1}\r\ntree planting: 3 vote(s)\r\nbook drive: 2 vote(s)\r\nbeach clean-up: 1 vote(s)\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis \"tally\" pattern appears again in the machine learning chapters, where we count labels in a dataset.\r\n\r\n## 🧾 A List of Dictionaries: A Small Table\r\n\r\nReal data often has many records with the same keys. Put each record in a dictionary and keep them all in a list. Each dictionary is one **row**; each key is one **column**.\r\n\r\n| id | name | age | priority |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| P-101 | Amina | 45 | High |\r\n| P-102 | Bilal | 12 | Normal |\r\n| P-103 | Khadija | 70 | Urgent |\r\n\r\n```python\r\nclinic_queue = [\r\n    {\"id\": \"P-101\", \"name\": \"Amina\", \"age\": 45, \"priority\": \"High\"},\r\n    {\"id\": \"P-102\", \"name\": \"Bilal\", \"age\": 12, \"priority\": \"Normal\"},\r\n    {\"id\": \"P-103\", \"name\": \"Khadija\", \"age\": 70, \"priority\": \"Urgent\"},\r\n    {\"id\": \"P-104\", \"name\": \"Omar\", \"age\": 28, \"priority\": \"Normal\"}\r\n]\r\n\r\nprint(\"PATIENTS TO SEE FIRST\")\r\nfor patient in clinic_queue:\r\n    if patient[\"priority\"] in [\"Urgent\", \"High\"]:\r\n        print(f\"{patient['id']}  {patient['name']}  ({patient['priority']})\")\r\n\r\nprint(\"First record's age:\", clinic_queue[0][\"age\"])\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nPATIENTS TO SEE FIRST\r\nP-101  Amina  (High)\r\nP-103  Khadija  (Urgent)\r\nFirst record's age: 45\r\n```\r\n\r\n`clinic_queue[0][\"age\"]` reads in two steps: first item of the list, then the `\"age\"` key. Inside a double-quoted f-string, use single quotes for keys: `{patient['name']}`.\r\n\r\n## ⭕ Sets: Only Unique Items\r\n\r\nA **set** is a collection where **no value can appear twice**. Write it with curly braces but no colons: `{\"a\", \"b\"}`. A set has **no order**, so it has no indexes. When printing a set of words, the order can change from run to run. So in this chapter we print `sorted(...)`. The built-in `sorted()` gives back a new, sorted **list**.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nattendees = [\"Hana\", \"Yusuf\", \"Hana\", \"Maryam\", \"Yusuf\", \"Hana\"]\r\n\r\nunique_people = set(attendees)\r\nprint(len(attendees), \"sign-ins\")\r\nprint(len(unique_people), \"different people\")\r\nprint(sorted(unique_people))\r\n\r\nunique_people.add(\"Idris\")\r\nprint(\"Idris\" in unique_people)\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\n6 sign-ins\r\n3 different people\r\n['Hana', 'Maryam', 'Yusuf']\r\nTrue\r\n```\r\n\r\n`set(list)` is a quick way to remove duplicates. `in` is very fast on a set.\r\n\r\n### Comparing Two Groups\r\n\r\n| Method | Meaning |\r\n| :--- | :--- |\r\n| `a.intersection(b)` | items in **both** sets |\r\n| `a.union(b)` | items in **either** set |\r\n| `a.difference(b)` | items in `a` but **not** in `b` |\r\n\r\n```python\r\ngroup_a = {\"Python\", \"SQL\", \"Git\"}\r\ngroup_b = {\"Python\", \"Statistics\", \"Git\", \"Ethics\"}\r\n\r\nprint(sorted(group_a.intersection(group_b)))\r\nprint(sorted(group_a.union(group_b)))\r\nprint(sorted(group_b.difference(group_a)))\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\n['Git', 'Python']\r\n['Ethics', 'Git', 'Python', 'SQL', 'Statistics']\r\n['Ethics', 'Statistics']\r\n```\r\n\r\n## 🧭 Which Collection Should I Use?\r\n\r\n| Collection | Looks like | Ordered? | Can change? | Duplicates? | Use for |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| List | `[1, 2]` | Yes | Yes | Yes | A register that grows |\r\n| Tuple | `(1, 2)` | Yes | No | Yes | Fixed facts |\r\n| Dictionary | `{\"a\": 1}` | Yes | Yes | Keys: no | Looking up by label |\r\n| Set | `{1, 2}` | No | Yes | No | Unique items |\r\n\r\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\r\n\r\n**1. Reading a key that does not exist.**\r\n\r\n```python\r\nstudent = {\"name\": \"Maryam\", \"grade\": 8}\r\nprint(student[\"email\"])\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nKeyError: 'email'\r\n```\r\n\r\nA `KeyError` means the key is not in the dictionary. **Fix:** check with `if \"email\" in student:` or use `student.get(\"email\", \"not given\")`. (Collecting only the details you really need, like no email for a reading log, also protects privacy: *Amānah*.)\r\n\r\n**2. Making an empty set with `{}`.** Empty braces make an empty **dictionary**, not a set.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nbadges = {}\r\nprint(type(badges))\r\nbadges = set()\r\nprint(type(badges))\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\n<class 'dict'>\r\n<class 'set'>\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Fix:** use `set()` for an empty set.\r\n\r\n## ✍️ Try It\r\n\r\nCreate a dictionary of a science-fair project: `title`, `team` (a list of two names), `category` and `score`. Print each key and value with `.items()`. Then raise the score by 5 and print the new score.\r\n\r\n**Hint:** `project[\"score\"] += 5` works on dictionary values too.\r\n\r\n## 📝 Recap\r\n\r\n- A dictionary stores key-value pairs: `{\"key\": value}`. Read with `d[\"key\"]` or, safely, `d.get(\"key\", default)`.\r\n- Assign to a key to add or change it; `.pop(key)` removes it; `in` checks keys.\r\n- `.items()` gives keys and values together in a loop. The `.get(x, 0) + 1` pattern counts things.\r\n- A list of dictionaries is a small table: one dictionary per row.\r\n- A set keeps only unique items and has no order. `set()` makes an empty set.\r\n\r\n## 🏆 Practice\r\n\r\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\r\nWhat does `{\"a\": 1, \"b\": 2}.get(\"c\", 0)` give? And what does `len({\"x\", \"y\", \"x\"})` give?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n`0` (the key `\"c\"` is missing, so the default is returned) and `2` (the duplicate `\"x\"` is removed).\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\r\nCount how many books of each subject were returned today:\r\n`returned = [\"science\", \"history\", \"science\", \"maths\", \"science\"]`. Print the dictionary.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```python\r\nreturned = [\"science\", \"history\", \"science\", \"maths\", \"science\"]\r\ncounts = {}\r\nfor subject in returned:\r\n    counts[subject] = counts.get(subject, 0) + 1\r\nprint(counts)\r\n```\r\n\r\nOutput:\r\n\r\n```text\r\n{'science': 3, 'history': 1, 'maths': 1}\r\n```\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\r\nA bakery donates bread to three shelters. The records are a list of dictionaries:\r\n\r\n```python\r\ndeliveries = [\r\n    {\"shelter\": \"North\", \"loaves\": 30},\r\n    {\"shelter\": \"East\", \"loaves\": 25},\r\n    {\"shelter\": \"North\", \"loaves\": 20},\r\n    {\"shelter\": \"West\", \"loaves\": 15}\r\n]\r\n```\r\n\r\n1. Build a dictionary with the total loaves for each shelter.\r\n2. Print each shelter and its total.\r\n3. Use a set to print how many different shelters received bread.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```python\r\ntotals = {}\r\nshelters = set()\r\n\r\nfor d in deliveries:\r\n    name = d[\"shelter\"]\r\n    totals[name] = totals.get(name, 0) + d[\"loaves\"]\r\n    shelters.add(name)\r\n\r\nfor name, loaves in totals.items():\r\n    print(f\"{name}: {loaves} loaves\")\r\nprint(\"Different shelters:\", len(shelters))\r\n```\r\n\r\nOutput:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nNorth: 50 loaves\r\nEast: 25 loaves\r\nWest: 15 loaves\r\nDifferent shelters: 3\r\n```\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n",
  "part-03-data-structures-and-functions/ch08-functions-and-modularity.md": "# Chapter 8: Functions & Modularity 🧩\r\n\r\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\r\n\r\nA halal bakery has a recipe card for date biscuits. The baker does not rewrite the recipe every morning. She takes out the card, follows the steps and gets the same good biscuits. If she wants a bigger batch, she changes one number: the amount of flour. A **function** is a recipe card for code. You write the steps once, give them a name, and use them whenever you need, with different ingredients each time.\r\n\r\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\r\n\r\n- Define your own functions with `def` and call them.\r\n- Pass information in with parameters, including default values and keyword arguments.\r\n- Send results back with `return`, and explain how it differs from `print()`.\r\n- Explain local and global scope.\r\n- Import ready-made modules such as `math` and `statistics`, and split your own code into modules.\r\n\r\n## 🔁 Why Functions? Don't Repeat Yourself\r\n\r\nLook at this code. The same three lines appear twice, with only the name changed.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nprint(\"-\" * 24)\r\nprint(\"Welcome, Hassan!\")\r\nprint(\"-\" * 24)\r\nprint(\"-\" * 24)\r\nprint(\"Welcome, Maryam!\")\r\nprint(\"-\" * 24)\r\n```\r\n\r\nIf the school asks for a new design, you must change it in every copy. Programmers follow the **DRY** principle: *Don't Repeat Yourself*. A function lets you write the steps **once**.\r\n\r\n## 🏗️ Defining and Calling a Function\r\n\r\nYou **define** a function with the keyword `def`, a name, round brackets and a colon. The indented lines below are the **body**: the steps the function runs.\r\n\r\n```python\r\ndef welcome(name):\r\n    print(\"-\" * 24)\r\n    print(f\"Welcome, {name}!\")\r\n    print(\"-\" * 24)\r\n\r\nwelcome(\"Hassan\")\r\nwelcome(\"Maryam\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\n------------------------\r\nWelcome, Hassan!\r\n------------------------\r\n------------------------\r\nWelcome, Maryam!\r\n------------------------\r\n```\r\n\r\n- Defining a function does **not** run it. It only teaches Python the recipe.\r\n- **Calling** the function runs it. You call it by writing its name and brackets: `welcome(\"Hassan\")`.\r\n- Define a function **before** the line that calls it, because Python reads from top to bottom.\r\n\r\n## 📦 Parameters and Arguments\r\n\r\nThe name inside the brackets of `def` is a **parameter**: a variable that waits for a value. The value you give when you call the function is an **argument**.\r\n\r\n| Word | Where you see it | Example |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| Parameter | In the `def` line | `def area(width, height):` |\r\n| Argument | In the call | `area(4, 3)` |\r\n\r\nA function can have several parameters, separated by commas. Arguments are matched **in order**:\r\n\r\n```python\r\ndef describe_room(room, seats, has_projector):\r\n    print(f\"Room {room}: {seats} seats, projector: {has_projector}\")\r\n\r\ndescribe_room(\"Lab 1\", 30, True)\r\ndescribe_room(\"Library\", 45, False)\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nRoom Lab 1: 30 seats, projector: True\r\nRoom Library: 45 seats, projector: False\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Keyword Arguments and Default Values\r\n\r\nYou can also name the parameter when you call: `seats=30`. These are **keyword arguments**. They make calls easier to read, and their order does not matter.\r\n\r\nA parameter can have a **default value**, written with `=` in the `def` line. If the caller leaves that argument out, the default is used.\r\n\r\n```python\r\ndef greet(name, title=\"Student\"):\r\n    print(f\"Welcome, {title} {name}! Strive with diligence today.\")\r\n\r\ngreet(\"Hassan\")\r\ngreet(\"Maryam\", title=\"Team Leader\")\r\ngreet(title=\"Teacher\", name=\"Ms Aisha\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nWelcome, Student Hassan! Strive with diligence today.\r\nWelcome, Team Leader Maryam! Strive with diligence today.\r\nWelcome, Teacher Ms Aisha! Strive with diligence today.\r\n```\r\n\r\nParameters with defaults must come **after** the ones without.\r\n\r\n## ↩️ Returning a Result with `return`\r\n\r\nSo far our functions only printed. Most useful functions **calculate** something and hand the answer back with `return`. The caller can store that answer, print it, or use it in more maths.\r\n\r\n```python\r\ndef rectangle_area(width, height):\r\n    return width * height\r\n\r\ngarden = rectangle_area(6, 4)\r\npath = rectangle_area(6, 1)\r\n\r\nprint(f\"Garden: {garden} square metres\")\r\nprint(f\"Path: {path} square metres\")\r\nprint(f\"Planting space: {garden - path} square metres\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nGarden: 24 square metres\r\nPath: 6 square metres\r\nPlanting space: 18 square metres\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe call `rectangle_area(6, 4)` is replaced by the value it returns, `24`. That is why we can do `garden - path`.\r\n\r\n### `return` versus `print()`\r\n\r\nThis difference confuses many beginners, so look closely.\r\n\r\n| | `print()` | `return` |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| What it does | Shows a value on the screen | Hands a value back to the caller |\r\n| Can the caller use the value? | No | Yes |\r\n| Ends the function? | No | Yes |\r\n\r\nA function with no `return` gives back `None`, which means \"nothing\":\r\n\r\n```python\r\ndef show_total(a, b):\r\n    print(a + b)\r\n\r\ndef get_total(a, b):\r\n    return a + b\r\n\r\nresult1 = show_total(3, 4)\r\nresult2 = get_total(3, 4)\r\nprint(\"result1 is\", result1)\r\nprint(\"result2 is\", result2)\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\n7\r\nresult1 is None\r\nresult2 is 7\r\n```\r\n\r\n`show_total` printed `7` itself, but gave nothing back, so `result1` is `None`.\r\n\r\n### `return` Ends the Function\r\n\r\nAs soon as Python reaches `return`, it leaves the function. This lets you return early from inside an `if`:\r\n\r\n```python\r\ndef grade_label(score):\r\n    if score >= 90:\r\n        return \"Distinction\"\r\n    if score >= 50:\r\n        return \"Pass\"\r\n    return \"Needs review\"\r\n\r\nprint(grade_label(95))\r\nprint(grade_label(67))\r\nprint(grade_label(30))\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nDistinction\r\nPass\r\nNeeds review\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Returning More Than One Value\r\n\r\nA function can return several values separated by commas. Python packs them into a **tuple** (Chapter 6). Unpack them into separate variables:\r\n\r\n```python\r\ndef lowest_and_highest(readings):\r\n    return min(readings), max(readings)\r\n\r\nlow, high = lowest_and_highest([18.5, 22.0, 25.5, 20.0])\r\nprint(f\"Lowest {low}, highest {high}\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nLowest 18.5, highest 25.5\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Docstrings: Explaining Your Function\r\n\r\nThe first line inside a function can be a string in triple quotes `\"\"\"...\"\"\"`. This is a **docstring**. It explains what the function does. Tools and teammates read it.\r\n\r\n```python\r\ndef average(numbers):\r\n    \"\"\"Return the average of a list of numbers.\"\"\"\r\n    return sum(numbers) / len(numbers)\r\n\r\nprint(round(average([88, 92, 79, 95]), 1))\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\n88.5\r\n```\r\n\r\n## 🌐 Scope: Where a Variable Lives\r\n\r\nThe **scope** of a variable is the part of the program where it can be used.\r\n\r\n- A variable created **inside** a function is **local**. It exists only while the function runs. Parameters are local too.\r\n- A variable created **outside** all functions is **global**. Functions can read it.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nschool = \"Al-Noor Academy\"      # global\r\n\r\ndef check_temperature():\r\n    reading = 28.5               # local\r\n    print(f\"{school}: {reading} degrees\")\r\n\r\ncheck_temperature()\r\nprint(school)\r\nprint(reading)\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nAl-Noor Academy: 28.5 degrees\r\nAl-Noor Academy\r\nNameError: name 'reading' is not defined\r\n```\r\n\r\nInside the function, both `school` and `reading` work. Outside, `reading` has gone, so Python reports a `NameError`.\r\n\r\n```\r\n┌ global ─────────────────────┐\r\n│ school = \"Al-Noor Academy\"  │\r\n│  ┌ check_temperature() ───┐ │\r\n│  │ reading = 28.5 (local) │ │\r\n│  └────────────────────────┘ │\r\n└─────────────────────────────┘\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Good habit:** pass values **in** as arguments and send results **out** with `return`. Functions that do not depend on global variables are easier to test and reuse.\r\n\r\n## 📚 Modules: Borrowing Ready-Made Functions\r\n\r\nA **module** is a file of Python code that other programs can use. Python comes with many modules, called the **standard library**. You load one with `import`.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nimport math\r\nimport statistics\r\n\r\nprint(math.sqrt(144))\r\nprint(round(math.pi, 4))\r\nprint(math.ceil(4.1))\r\nprint(math.floor(4.9))\r\n\r\nheights_cm = [12.0, 15.5, 11.0, 14.5, 13.0]\r\nprint(statistics.mean(heights_cm))\r\nprint(statistics.median(heights_cm))\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\n12.0\r\n3.1416\r\n5\r\n4\r\n13.2\r\n13.0\r\n```\r\n\r\n| Function | What it does | Example |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| `math.sqrt(x)` | Square root | `math.sqrt(144)` → `12.0` |\r\n| `math.ceil(x)` | Rounds **up** to a whole number | `math.ceil(4.1)` → `5` |\r\n| `math.floor(x)` | Rounds **down** | `math.floor(4.9)` → `4` |\r\n| `statistics.mean(x)` | Average of a list | `13.2` above |\r\n| `statistics.median(x)` | Middle value when sorted | `13.0` above |\r\n\r\nYou write `math.sqrt` with the module name first, so it is clear where the function came from. If you only need one function, you can import it by name:\r\n\r\n```python\r\nfrom math import ceil\r\n\r\nstudents = 130\r\nbus_seats = 40\r\nprint(f\"Buses needed: {ceil(students / bus_seats)}\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nBuses needed: 4\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Making Your Own Module\r\n\r\nWhen a program grows, you can move functions into their own file. Any `.py` file is a module. Suppose you save this as `converters.py`:\r\n\r\n```python\r\n# converters.py\r\ndef celsius_to_fahrenheit(celsius):\r\n    \"\"\"Convert a Celsius temperature to Fahrenheit.\"\"\"\r\n    return celsius * 9 / 5 + 32\r\n\r\ndef rain_mm_to_litres(rain_mm, roof_area):\r\n    \"\"\"1 mm of rain on 1 square metre is 1 litre.\"\"\"\r\n    return rain_mm * roof_area\r\n```\r\n\r\nIn another file in the **same folder**, for example `report.py`, import it by its file name without `.py`:\r\n\r\n```python\r\n# report.py\r\nimport converters\r\n\r\nprint(converters.celsius_to_fahrenheit(24.0))\r\nprint(converters.rain_mm_to_litres(18.5, 40))\r\n```\r\n\r\nRunning `python report.py` on a computer prints:\r\n\r\n```text\r\n75.2\r\n740.0\r\n```\r\n\r\nSplitting code into modules like this is called **modularity**. Each file has one clear job, which makes big programs easier to understand and share.\r\n\r\n## 🤲 Worked Example: A Zakat Helper\r\n\r\n*Zakat* is the yearly charity of 2.5% on savings that reach the **nisab**, a minimum amount. The real nisab depends on the current price of gold or silver, so it changes. The number below is only a **practice value** for this exercise.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nNISAB_PRACTICE = 5000.0     # practice value only\r\n\r\ndef zakat_due(savings, nisab=NISAB_PRACTICE):\r\n    \"\"\"Return 2.5% of savings if they reach the nisab, else 0.\"\"\"\r\n    if savings >= nisab:\r\n        return savings * 0.025\r\n    return 0.0\r\n\r\nfor savings in [3000.0, 5000.0, 12000.0]:\r\n    due = zakat_due(savings)\r\n    print(f\"Savings {savings}: zakat {round(due, 2)}\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nSavings 3000.0: zakat 0.0\r\nSavings 5000.0: zakat 125.0\r\nSavings 12000.0: zakat 300.0\r\n```\r\n\r\nA name written in CAPITAL letters, like `NISAB_PRACTICE`, is a habit that tells readers \"this value is meant to stay the same\". It is called a **constant**.\r\n\r\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\r\n\r\n**1. Printing instead of returning.** The function shows a number, but the caller gets `None` and cannot do maths with it.\r\n\r\n```python\r\ndef double(n):\r\n    print(n * 2)\r\n\r\nanswer = double(5)\r\nprint(answer + 1)\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\n10\r\nTypeError: unsupported operand type(s) for +: 'NoneType' and 'int'\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe `10` was printed, then the program crashed: you cannot add `None` and `1`. **Fix:** write `return n * 2` instead of `print(n * 2)`.\r\n\r\n**2. Forgetting the brackets when calling.** `welcome` without `()` does not run the function. It only names it. Always call with brackets: `welcome(\"Hassan\")`.\r\n\r\n## ✍️ Try It\r\n\r\nWrite a function `minutes_to_hours(minutes)` that **returns** the hours, rounded to 2 decimal places. Call it for 90, 45 and 200 minutes and print each result.\r\n\r\n**Hint:** `return round(minutes / 60, 2)`. The answers are 1.5, 0.75 and 3.33.\r\n\r\n## 📝 Recap\r\n\r\n- `def name(parameters):` defines a function; `name(arguments)` calls it.\r\n- Parameters can have default values; keyword arguments name the parameter in the call.\r\n- `return` hands a value back and ends the function. Without `return`, a function gives `None`.\r\n- Variables made inside a function are local; they disappear when it ends.\r\n- `import math` or `from math import ceil` borrows ready-made functions. Your own `.py` files can be modules too.\r\n\r\n## 🏆 Practice\r\n\r\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\r\nIn `def area(width, height):` and `area(5, 2)`, which are the parameters and which are the arguments? What does a function return if it has no `return` line?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n`width` and `height` are **parameters**. `5` and `2` are **arguments**. A function with no `return` gives back `None`.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\r\nWrite `count_passes(scores, pass_mark=50)` that returns how many scores are at or above the pass mark. Test it with `[45, 80, 50, 32, 91]` and also with `pass_mark=60`.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```python\r\ndef count_passes(scores, pass_mark=50):\r\n    count = 0\r\n    for s in scores:\r\n        if s >= pass_mark:\r\n            count += 1\r\n    return count\r\n\r\nresults = [45, 80, 50, 32, 91]\r\nprint(count_passes(results))\r\nprint(count_passes(results, pass_mark=60))\r\n```\r\n\r\nOutput:\r\n\r\n```text\r\n3\r\n2\r\n```\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\r\nBuild a small water-tank toolkit for a school garden.\r\n\r\n1. Write `tank_volume(length, width, depth)` that returns the volume in litres. (1 cubic metre = 1000 litres, and the sizes are in metres.)\r\n2. Write `days_of_water(litres, litres_per_day=150)` that returns how many **whole** days the water lasts. Use `//`.\r\n3. Write `report(length, width, depth)` that calls the first two functions and prints one tidy line.\r\n4. Call `report(2, 1.5, 1)` and `report(1, 1, 0.5)`.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```python\r\ndef tank_volume(length, width, depth):\r\n    \"\"\"Return the tank volume in litres (sizes in metres).\"\"\"\r\n    return length * width * depth * 1000\r\n\r\ndef days_of_water(litres, litres_per_day=150):\r\n    \"\"\"Return how many whole days the water lasts.\"\"\"\r\n    return int(litres // litres_per_day)\r\n\r\ndef report(length, width, depth):\r\n    litres = tank_volume(length, width, depth)\r\n    days = days_of_water(litres)\r\n    print(f\"Tank holds {litres} litres: enough for {days} days\")\r\n\r\nreport(2, 1.5, 1)\r\nreport(1, 1, 0.5)\r\n```\r\n\r\nOutput:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nTank holds 3000.0 litres: enough for 20 days\r\nTank holds 500.0 litres: enough for 3 days\r\n```\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n",
  "part-04-data-and-files/ch09-working-with-files.md": "# Chapter 9: Working with Files & Context Managers 📁\r\n\r\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\r\n\r\nIn the science lab, each experiment is written in a logbook. When the bell rings, students go home, but the logbook stays on the shelf. Next week, the class opens it and carries on. A Python program is like a lesson that ends at the bell. When it stops, all its variables are wiped from memory. To keep information for next time, a program writes it into a **file**, the computer's logbook.\r\n\r\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\r\n\r\n- Open a file with `open()` and choose a mode: read, write or append.\r\n- Use `with` so that files are always closed safely.\r\n- Write lines of text, then read them back all at once or line by line.\r\n- Split each line into pieces of data and calculate with them.\r\n- Recognise `FileNotFoundError` and avoid wiping a file by accident.\r\n\r\n## 💡 Open, Use, Close\r\n\r\nWorking with a file is like working with a real notebook:\r\n\r\n1. **Open** the notebook: `open()`.\r\n2. **Read** or **write** on its pages.\r\n3. **Close** it so nothing gets damaged.\r\n\r\nIf a program forgets to close a file, some of the text may never be saved. Python has a safe way to make sure that never happens: the `with` statement.\r\n\r\n> 💻 **Where files are saved**: on a computer, files are saved in the folder you run Python from. In the Python Console on this website, files live in the browser's temporary memory. They work the same way, but disappear when you reload the page.\r\n\r\n## 📂 File Modes\r\n\r\nThe second argument of `open()` is the **mode**. It says what you plan to do.\r\n\r\n| Mode | Name | What it does | If the file does not exist |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| `\"r\"` | Read | Reads the file | Error: `FileNotFoundError` |\r\n| `\"w\"` | Write | **Empties** the file, then writes | Creates it |\r\n| `\"a\"` | Append | Adds to the **end**, keeps old text | Creates it |\r\n\r\nWe also pass `encoding=\"utf-8\"`. An **encoding** is the rule for storing letters as numbers. UTF-8 can store every language, including Arabic, Urdu and emoji.\r\n\r\n## ✍️ Writing a File with `with`\r\n\r\n```python\r\nwith open(\"club_log.txt\", \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as file:\r\n    file.write(\"Science Club Log\\n\")\r\n    file.write(\"Date: 2026-09-25\\n\")\r\n    file.write(\"Study kits given out: 150\\n\")\r\n\r\nprint(\"File written and closed.\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nFile written and closed.\r\n```\r\n\r\n- `with open(...) as file:` opens the file and calls it `file`. This is called a **context manager**.\r\n- When the indented block ends, Python **closes the file automatically**, even if something goes wrong.\r\n- `.write()` does **not** add a new line for you. The `\\n` at the end of each string means \"new line\". It is called a **newline character**.\r\n\r\n## 📖 Reading a File\r\n\r\n### All at Once with `.read()`\r\n\r\n`.read()` gives back the whole file as one string.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nwith open(\"club_log.txt\", \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as file:\r\n    content = file.read()\r\n\r\nprint(content)\r\nprint(\"Characters in file:\", len(content))\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nScience Club Log\r\nDate: 2026-09-25\r\nStudy kits given out: 150\r\n\r\nCharacters in file: 60\r\n```\r\n\r\nThere is a blank line before \"Characters\": the file ends with `\\n`, and `print()` adds another.\r\n\r\n### Line by Line with a `for` Loop\r\n\r\nA `for` loop over a file gives one line at a time. This is the best way for big files, because Python never holds the whole file in memory.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nline_number = 0\r\n\r\nwith open(\"club_log.txt\", \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as file:\r\n    for line in file:\r\n        line_number += 1\r\n        print(f\"Line {line_number}: {line.strip()}\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nLine 1: Science Club Log\r\nLine 2: Date: 2026-09-25\r\nLine 3: Study kits given out: 150\r\n```\r\n\r\nEach `line` still ends with `\\n`. `.strip()` removes it. Without `.strip()`, you would see an empty line after every line.\r\n\r\n## ➕ Appending: Adding Without Erasing\r\n\r\nMode `\"a\"` adds new text at the end and keeps everything already there.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nwith open(\"club_log.txt\", \"a\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as file:\r\n    file.write(\"Volunteers: 12\\n\")\r\n\r\nwith open(\"club_log.txt\", \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as file:\r\n    print(file.read())\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nScience Club Log\r\nDate: 2026-09-25\r\nStudy kits given out: 150\r\nVolunteers: 12\r\n```\r\n\r\n## 🔢 Turning Lines into Data\r\n\r\nFiles often store one record per line, with values separated by commas. The string method `.split(\",\")` cuts a line into a **list** of pieces.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nline = \"Amina,12,science\\n\"\r\nparts = line.strip().split(\",\")\r\nprint(parts)\r\nprint(parts[0], int(parts[1]) + 1)\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\n['Amina', '12', 'science']\r\nAmina 13\r\n```\r\n\r\nEvery piece is a **string**, so numbers must be converted with `int()` or `float()`. Now we can total a whole file:\r\n\r\n```python\r\nwith open(\"reading_log.txt\", \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as file:\r\n    file.write(\"Amina,12\\n\")\r\n    file.write(\"Bilal,30\\n\")\r\n    file.write(\"Hana,18\\n\")\r\n\r\ntotal_pages = 0\r\nwith open(\"reading_log.txt\", \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as file:\r\n    for line in file:\r\n        name, pages = line.strip().split(\",\")\r\n        total_pages += int(pages)\r\n        print(f\"{name} read {pages} pages\")\r\n\r\nprint(f\"Class total: {total_pages} pages\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nAmina read 12 pages\r\nBilal read 30 pages\r\nHana read 18 pages\r\nClass total: 60 pages\r\n```\r\n\r\n`name, pages = ...` unpacks the two-item list into two variables, just like a tuple. In the next chapter, the `csv` module will do this splitting for us.\r\n\r\n## 🚫 When the File Is Missing\r\n\r\nReading a file that does not exist stops the program:\r\n\r\n```python\r\nwith open(\"missing_data.txt\", \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as file:\r\n    print(file.read())\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nFileNotFoundError: [Errno 2] No such file or directory: 'missing_data.txt'\r\n```\r\n\r\nCheck the file name and the folder first. As a short preview, Python can also **catch** an error with `try` and `except`, and run a friendly message instead of crashing:\r\n\r\n```python\r\ntry:\r\n    with open(\"missing_data.txt\", \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as file:\r\n        print(file.read())\r\nexcept FileNotFoundError:\r\n    print(\"No data file yet. Starting with an empty log.\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nNo data file yet. Starting with an empty log.\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe code under `try` runs first. If that exact error happens, Python jumps to the `except` block. You only need this simple form for now.\r\n\r\n## 📓 Worked Example: Daily Reflection Journal\r\n\r\nA student keeps a short daily journal of what she learned. On a computer, the entries could come from `input()`. Here they are in a list of tuples. Append mode keeps every old entry.\r\n\r\n```python\r\njournal = \"reflections.txt\"\r\n\r\nentries = [\r\n    (\"2026-09-24\", \"for loops\", \"my teacher's patience\"),\r\n    (\"2026-09-25\", \"writing files\", \"good health\")\r\n]\r\n\r\nfor date, learned, grateful in entries:\r\n    with open(journal, \"a\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as file:\r\n        file.write(f\"[{date}]\\n\")\r\n        file.write(f\"Learned  : {learned}\\n\")\r\n        file.write(f\"Grateful : {grateful}\\n\")\r\n        file.write(\"-\" * 24 + \"\\n\")\r\n\r\nwith open(journal, \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as file:\r\n    print(file.read())\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\n[2026-09-24]\r\nLearned  : for loops\r\nGrateful : my teacher's patience\r\n------------------------\r\n[2026-09-25]\r\nLearned  : writing files\r\nGrateful : good health\r\n------------------------\r\n```\r\n\r\nA personal journal is private. Store it only on your own device, and never share another person's notes without permission (*Amānah*).\r\n\r\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\r\n\r\n**1. Using `\"w\"` when you meant `\"a\"`.** Write mode empties the file first, so the old records are lost.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nwith open(\"visits.txt\", \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\r\n    f.write(\"Monday: 40 visitors\\n\")\r\n\r\nwith open(\"visits.txt\", \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\r\n    f.write(\"Tuesday: 35 visitors\\n\")\r\n\r\nwith open(\"visits.txt\", \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\r\n    print(f.read())\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nTuesday: 35 visitors\r\n```\r\n\r\nMonday's line has gone! **Fix:** open the second time with `\"a\"` to add to the end.\r\n\r\n**2. Forgetting `\\n`.** `file.write(\"Line 1\")` then `file.write(\"Line 2\")` saves `Line 1Line 2` on one line. **Fix:** end each written line with `\\n`.\r\n\r\n## ✍️ Try It\r\n\r\nWrite three of your weekly goals to `goals.txt`, one per line. Then read the file back and print each goal with a number in front, like `1. Read 20 pages a day`.\r\n\r\n**Hint:** write with mode `\"w\"` and `\\n`; read with a `for` loop, a counter and `.strip()`.\r\n\r\n## 📝 Recap\r\n\r\n- Files keep data after a program ends. Open them with `open(name, mode, encoding=\"utf-8\")`.\r\n- `\"r\"` reads, `\"w\"` empties then writes, `\"a\"` adds to the end.\r\n- `with open(...) as file:` closes the file automatically.\r\n- `.read()` gives the whole file; a `for` loop gives one line at a time. Use `.strip()` to remove `\\n`.\r\n- `.split(\",\")` turns a line into a list of strings; convert numbers with `int()` or `float()`.\r\n\r\n## 🏆 Practice\r\n\r\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\r\nWhich mode would you use to (a) add a new line to an existing log, (b) start a brand-new report, (c) only look at a file?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n(a) `\"a\"` append, (b) `\"w\"` write, (c) `\"r\"` read.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\r\nWrite `\"apples\\n\"`, `\"bread\\n\"` and `\"rice\\n\"` to `pantry.txt`. Then read the file and print how many lines it has.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```python\r\nwith open(\"pantry.txt\", \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\r\n    f.write(\"apples\\n\")\r\n    f.write(\"bread\\n\")\r\n    f.write(\"rice\\n\")\r\n\r\ncount = 0\r\nwith open(\"pantry.txt\", \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\r\n    for line in f:\r\n        count += 1\r\nprint(\"Lines:\", count)\r\n```\r\n\r\nOutput:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nLines: 3\r\n```\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\r\nA weather station saves one reading per line as `day,temperature`.\r\n\r\n1. Write these lines to `temps.txt`: `Sat,31`, `Sun,34`, `Mon,29`, `Tue,36`.\r\n2. Read the file. Split each line and convert the temperature to an `int`.\r\n3. Print the hottest day and its temperature, and the average temperature rounded to 1 decimal place.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```python\r\nwith open(\"temps.txt\", \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\r\n    f.write(\"Sat,31\\nSun,34\\nMon,29\\nTue,36\\n\")\r\n\r\nhottest_day = \"\"\r\nhottest = -100\r\ntotal = 0\r\ndays = 0\r\n\r\nwith open(\"temps.txt\", \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\r\n    for line in f:\r\n        day, temp = line.strip().split(\",\")\r\n        temp = int(temp)\r\n        total += temp\r\n        days += 1\r\n        if temp > hottest:\r\n            hottest = temp\r\n            hottest_day = day\r\n\r\nprint(f\"Hottest: {hottest_day} at {hottest} degrees\")\r\nprint(f\"Average: {round(total / days, 1)} degrees\")\r\n```\r\n\r\nOutput:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nHottest: Tue at 36 degrees\r\nAverage: 32.5 degrees\r\n```\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n",
  "part-04-data-and-files/ch10-tabular-data-csv-and-json.md": "# Chapter 10: Tabular Data (CSV & JSON) 📊\r\n\r\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\r\n\r\nTwo schools want to share their recycling results. One school writes a long paragraph: \"On Monday we collected about twelve kilos, and on Tuesday…\". The other sends a neat table with columns for day and kilograms. Which one can a computer read? The table, of course. Data science runs on **structured data**: information arranged in rows, columns and labels. The two most common formats for it are **CSV** and **JSON**.\r\n\r\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\r\n\r\n- Describe the CSV and JSON formats and when to use each.\r\n- Write and read CSV files with Python's `csv` module.\r\n- Convert CSV text values into numbers and calculate with them.\r\n- Save Python dictionaries and lists as JSON and load them back with the `json` module.\r\n\r\n## 📄 CSV: Comma-Separated Values\r\n\r\nA **CSV** file is plain text. Each line is one **row**. Commas separate the **columns**. The first line is usually the **header**: the names of the columns.\r\n\r\n```csv\r\ncity,solar_panels,daily_kwh\r\nCairo,120,480.5\r\nAmman,80,310.2\r\nTunis,95,390.0\r\n```\r\n\r\nAs a table, it looks like this:\r\n\r\n| city | solar_panels | daily_kwh |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| Cairo | 120 | 480.5 |\r\n| Amman | 80 | 310.2 |\r\n| Tunis | 95 | 390.0 |\r\n\r\nAny spreadsheet program can open a CSV file. That makes CSV a common way to move data between programs.\r\n\r\n### Writing a CSV File\r\n\r\nPython's built-in `csv` module handles the commas for you. `csv.writer` turns a list into one CSV row with `.writerow()`.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nimport csv\r\n\r\nwith open(\"solar.csv\", \"w\", newline=\"\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as file:\r\n    writer = csv.writer(file)\r\n    writer.writerow([\"city\", \"solar_panels\", \"daily_kwh\"])\r\n    writer.writerow([\"Cairo\", 120, 480.5])\r\n    writer.writerow([\"Amman\", 80, 310.2])\r\n    writer.writerow([\"Tunis\", 95, 390.0])\r\n\r\nwith open(\"solar.csv\", \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as file:\r\n    print(file.read())\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\ncity,solar_panels,daily_kwh\r\nCairo,120,480.5\r\nAmman,80,310.2\r\nTunis,95,390.0\r\n```\r\n\r\nAlways pass `newline=\"\"` when you open a CSV file for writing. Without it, Windows computers can add an extra blank line after every row.\r\n\r\n### Reading Rows as Lists: `csv.reader`\r\n\r\n`csv.reader` gives each row as a **list** of strings.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nimport csv\r\n\r\nwith open(\"solar.csv\", \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as file:\r\n    reader = csv.reader(file)\r\n    for row in reader:\r\n        print(row)\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\n['city', 'solar_panels', 'daily_kwh']\r\n['Cairo', '120', '480.5']\r\n['Amman', '80', '310.2']\r\n['Tunis', '95', '390.0']\r\n```\r\n\r\nNotice the quotes: `'120'` is a **string**. Everything read from a CSV file is text.\r\n\r\n### Reading Rows as Dictionaries: `csv.DictReader`\r\n\r\n`csv.DictReader` is often easier. It reads the header for you and gives each row as a **dictionary**. You use the column names as keys.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nimport csv\r\n\r\ntotal_kwh = 0\r\nwith open(\"solar.csv\", \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as file:\r\n    reader = csv.DictReader(file)\r\n    for row in reader:\r\n        energy = float(row[\"daily_kwh\"])\r\n        total_kwh += energy\r\n        print(f\"{row['city']}: {energy} kWh from {row['solar_panels']} panels\")\r\n\r\nprint(f\"Total: {round(total_kwh, 1)} kWh per day\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nCairo: 480.5 kWh from 120 panels\r\nAmman: 310.2 kWh from 80 panels\r\nTunis: 390.0 kWh from 95 panels\r\nTotal: 1180.7 kWh per day\r\n```\r\n\r\n| Tool | Each row is a… | Read a value with |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| `csv.reader` | list | `row[2]` |\r\n| `csv.DictReader` | dictionary | `row[\"daily_kwh\"]` |\r\n\r\n## 🌐 JSON: Data with Labels and Nesting\r\n\r\n**JSON** (JavaScript Object Notation) stores data with labels, like a Python dictionary. It can also hold lists **inside** records, which a flat CSV table cannot do easily. Websites and apps use JSON to send data to each other.\r\n\r\n```json\r\n{\r\n  \"experiment\": \"Plant growth under LED light\",\r\n  \"batch_id\": 402,\r\n  \"samples\": [\r\n    {\"plant_id\": \"P1\", \"height_cm\": 14.2, \"healthy\": true},\r\n    {\"plant_id\": \"P2\", \"height_cm\": 11.8, \"healthy\": false}\r\n  ]\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nJSON looks almost like Python, with a few small differences:\r\n\r\n| Python | JSON |\r\n| :--- | :--- |\r\n| `dict` `{\"a\": 1}` | object `{\"a\": 1}` |\r\n| `list` `[1, 2]` | array `[1, 2]` |\r\n| `True` / `False` | `true` / `false` |\r\n| `None` | `null` |\r\n| `'single'` or `\"double\"` quotes | `\"double\"` quotes only |\r\n\r\n### Saving and Loading JSON\r\n\r\nThe `json` module converts in both directions:\r\n\r\n- `json.dump(data, file)` saves Python data into a JSON file.\r\n- `json.load(file)` reads a JSON file back into Python data.\r\n- The argument `indent=2` adds line breaks and spaces so people can read the file.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nimport json\r\n\r\nfarm = {\r\n    \"organisation\": \"Green Oasis Farm Co-op\",\r\n    \"acres\": 25.5,\r\n    \"crops\": [\"barley\", \"olives\", \"pomegranates\"],\r\n    \"organic\": True,\r\n    \"manager\": None\r\n}\r\n\r\nwith open(\"farm.json\", \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as file:\r\n    json.dump(farm, file, indent=2)\r\n\r\nwith open(\"farm.json\", \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as file:\r\n    print(file.read())\r\n\r\nwith open(\"farm.json\", \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as file:\r\n    loaded = json.load(file)\r\n\r\nprint(loaded[\"crops\"][1])\r\nprint(type(loaded))\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\n{\r\n  \"organisation\": \"Green Oasis Farm Co-op\",\r\n  \"acres\": 25.5,\r\n  \"crops\": [\r\n    \"barley\",\r\n    \"olives\",\r\n    \"pomegranates\"\r\n  ],\r\n  \"organic\": true,\r\n  \"manager\": null\r\n}\r\nolives\r\n<class 'dict'>\r\n```\r\n\r\nIn the file, `True` became `true` and `None` became `null`. After `json.load`, they are Python values again, and `loaded` is a normal dictionary.\r\n\r\n### JSON from a String\r\n\r\nData from a website often arrives as a string of JSON text. `json.loads()` (with an **s** for \"string\") turns that text into Python data. `json.dumps()` does the reverse.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nimport json\r\n\r\nmessage = '{\"station\": \"North Field\", \"readings\": [21.5, 23.0, 22.5]}'\r\ndata = json.loads(message)\r\n\r\nreadings = data[\"readings\"]\r\nprint(data[\"station\"])\r\nprint(f\"Average: {round(sum(readings) / len(readings), 2)}\")\r\nprint(json.dumps({\"ok\": True, \"count\": 3}))\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nNorth Field\r\nAverage: 22.33\r\n{\"ok\": true, \"count\": 3}\r\n```\r\n\r\n## 🆚 CSV or JSON?\r\n\r\n| | CSV | JSON |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| Shape | Flat table: rows and columns | Nested: records inside records |\r\n| Types | Everything is text | Numbers, true/false, null, text |\r\n| Opens in a spreadsheet | Yes | No |\r\n| Common use | Sensor logs, gradebooks | Web apps, settings, APIs |\r\n\r\n## 📈 Looking Ahead: NumPy and Pandas\r\n\r\nData scientists often use two extra libraries. They are not part of standard Python; on a computer you install them with `pip install pandas`.\r\n\r\n- **NumPy**: fast maths on large lists of numbers.\r\n- **Pandas**: tables called **DataFrames**, a bit like a spreadsheet inside Python.\r\n\r\n```python\r\n# A preview only: this needs pandas installed\r\n# import pandas as pd\r\n# table = pd.read_csv(\"solar.csv\")\r\n# print(table[\"daily_kwh\"].mean())\r\n```\r\n\r\nEverything in this book uses only the standard library, so you understand what these tools do for you.\r\n\r\n## 📚 Worked Example: Library Borrowing Report\r\n\r\nThe school library stores how often each book was borrowed. We write the records with `csv.DictWriter`, then read them back to find the most borrowed book.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nimport csv\r\n\r\ncatalogue = [\r\n    {\"isbn\": \"978-01\", \"title\": \"Algorithmic Integrity\", \"borrows\": 45},\r\n    {\"isbn\": \"978-02\", \"title\": \"History of Mathematics\", \"borrows\": 62},\r\n    {\"isbn\": \"978-03\", \"title\": \"Intro to Python and AI\", \"borrows\": 110},\r\n    {\"isbn\": \"978-04\", \"title\": \"Principles of Astronomy\", \"borrows\": 38}\r\n]\r\n\r\nwith open(\"library.csv\", \"w\", newline=\"\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\r\n    writer = csv.DictWriter(f, fieldnames=[\"isbn\", \"title\", \"borrows\"])\r\n    writer.writeheader()\r\n    writer.writerows(catalogue)\r\n\r\ntop_title = \"\"\r\ntop_borrows = 0\r\ntotal = 0\r\n\r\nwith open(\"library.csv\", \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\r\n    for row in csv.DictReader(f):\r\n        borrows = int(row[\"borrows\"])\r\n        total += borrows\r\n        if borrows > top_borrows:\r\n            top_borrows = borrows\r\n            top_title = row[\"title\"]\r\n\r\nprint(f\"Most borrowed: {top_title} ({top_borrows} times)\")\r\nprint(f\"All borrows this term: {total}\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nMost borrowed: Intro to Python and AI (110 times)\r\nAll borrows this term: 255\r\n```\r\n\r\n`.writeheader()` writes the column names. `.writerows()` writes every dictionary in the list as a row.\r\n\r\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\r\n\r\n**1. Comparing CSV numbers while they are still text.** Strings are compared letter by letter, like words in a dictionary, so `\"110\"` comes **before** `\"45\"`.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nborrows_from_csv = [\"45\", \"62\", \"110\", \"38\"]\r\nprint(max(borrows_from_csv))\r\n\r\nnumbers = []\r\nfor b in borrows_from_csv:\r\n    numbers.append(int(b))\r\nprint(max(numbers))\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\n62\r\n110\r\n```\r\n\r\n`\"62\"` won because `\"6\"` comes after `\"4\"`, `\"3\"` and `\"1\"`. **Fix:** convert with `int()` or `float()` before comparing or adding.\r\n\r\n**2. Writing Python spellings inside JSON text.** JSON needs `true` and double quotes.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nimport json\r\njson.loads(\"{'done': True}\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\njson.decoder.JSONDecodeError: Expecting property name enclosed in double quotes: line 1 column 2 (char 1)\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Fix:** write `'{\"done\": true}'`, or build a Python dictionary and let `json.dumps()` write the JSON for you.\r\n\r\n## ✍️ Try It\r\n\r\nCreate a CSV file `cleanup.csv` with the header `street,bags` and three streets with bag counts. Read it with `csv.DictReader` and print the total number of bags.\r\n\r\n**Hint:** remember `newline=\"\"` when writing, and `int(row[\"bags\"])` when reading.\r\n\r\n## 📝 Recap\r\n\r\n- CSV stores a flat table as text: one row per line, commas between columns, a header first.\r\n- `csv.writer` / `csv.DictWriter` write rows; `csv.reader` / `csv.DictReader` read them.\r\n- Every value read from CSV is a string. Convert numbers before doing maths or comparing.\r\n- JSON stores labelled, nested data. `json.dump`/`json.load` work with files; `json.dumps`/`json.loads` work with strings.\r\n- In JSON: `true`, `false`, `null` and double quotes only.\r\n\r\n## 🏆 Practice\r\n\r\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\r\nHow do you write the Python value `None` in JSON? What type is every value that `csv.DictReader` gives you?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n`None` is written as `null`. Every value from `csv.DictReader` is a **string** (`str`).\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\r\nTurn this dictionary into JSON text with `json.dumps` and print it:\r\n`{\"club\": \"Robotics\", \"members\": 14, \"active\": True}`.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```python\r\nimport json\r\nclub = {\"club\": \"Robotics\", \"members\": 14, \"active\": True}\r\nprint(json.dumps(club))\r\n```\r\n\r\nOutput:\r\n\r\n```text\r\n{\"club\": \"Robotics\", \"members\": 14, \"active\": true}\r\n```\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\r\nConvert a CSV gradebook into a JSON report.\r\n\r\n1. Write `grades.csv` with columns `name,score` and four students: Amina 88, Bilal 74, Hana 95, Idris 61.\r\n2. Read it with `csv.DictReader`. Build a list of dictionaries with `score` as an `int`.\r\n3. Work out the class average.\r\n4. Save `{\"average\": ..., \"students\": [...]}` to `report.json` with `indent=2`, then load it and print the average.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```python\r\nimport csv\r\nimport json\r\n\r\nwith open(\"grades.csv\", \"w\", newline=\"\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\r\n    writer = csv.writer(f)\r\n    writer.writerow([\"name\", \"score\"])\r\n    writer.writerow([\"Amina\", 88])\r\n    writer.writerow([\"Bilal\", 74])\r\n    writer.writerow([\"Hana\", 95])\r\n    writer.writerow([\"Idris\", 61])\r\n\r\nstudents = []\r\nwith open(\"grades.csv\", \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\r\n    for row in csv.DictReader(f):\r\n        students.append({\"name\": row[\"name\"], \"score\": int(row[\"score\"])})\r\n\r\ntotal = 0\r\nfor s in students:\r\n    total += s[\"score\"]\r\naverage = round(total / len(students), 1)\r\n\r\nwith open(\"report.json\", \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\r\n    json.dump({\"average\": average, \"students\": students}, f, indent=2)\r\n\r\nwith open(\"report.json\", \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\r\n    report = json.load(f)\r\nprint(\"Class average:\", report[\"average\"])\r\n```\r\n\r\nOutput:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nClass average: 79.5\r\n```\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n",
  "part-05-machine-learning-foundations/ch11-what-is-machine-learning.md": "# Chapter 11: What is Machine Learning? 🧠\r\n\r\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\r\n\r\nWhen Zainab was small, her grandmother took her to the date market. Her grandmother never gave her a list of rules for spotting ripe dates. Instead, she showed her many dates: \"This one is ripe. This one is not yet.\" After seeing enough examples, Zainab could judge new dates by herself. She had **learned from examples**. Machine learning is a way for computers to do the same: find a pattern in many examples, then use it on new cases.\r\n\r\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\r\n\r\n- Explain the difference between writing rules and learning from data.\r\n- Use the key words: dataset, feature, label, model, training and prediction.\r\n- Store a small dataset in Python and separate its features from its labels.\r\n- Watch a tiny model \"learn\" by trying guesses and measuring its error.\r\n- Describe what machine learning cannot do, and why ethics matters from day one.\r\n\r\n## 💡 Rules versus Learning\r\n\r\nUntil now, you wrote **rules-based programs**. You told the computer exactly what to do:\r\n\r\n- \"If moisture is below 30, switch on the water.\"\r\n- \"If the score is 90 or more, print Distinction.\"\r\n\r\nThat works when the rules are clear. But how would you write `if` rules to tell a healthy olive leaf from a sick one in a photo? There are millions of pixels and shadows. Nobody can write all those rules by hand.\r\n\r\n**Machine learning (ML)** turns the job around. We give the computer examples **and** their answers. It works out the rule itself.\r\n\r\n```\r\nNormal programming:\r\n data + rules  ──>  answers\r\n\r\nMachine learning:\r\n data + answers ──>  rules (a model)\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe rule that the computer finds is called a **model**. Once we have a model, we can give it new data and it produces answers.\r\n\r\n## 🏷️ The Key Words of ML\r\n\r\n| Word | Meaning | Example (plant leaves) |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| **Dataset** | A collection of examples | 500 leaf measurements |\r\n| **Feature** | A measured input about each example | greenness, number of spots |\r\n| **Label** | The correct answer for an example | \"healthy\" or \"sick\" |\r\n| **Model** | The pattern learned from the data | \"many spots usually means sick\" |\r\n| **Training** | The process of learning from the dataset | trying rules and fixing mistakes |\r\n| **Prediction** | The model's answer for a new example | \"this new leaf is probably healthy\" |\r\n\r\nEngineers often call the features **X** and the label **y**. Here is a small, made-up leaf dataset:\r\n\r\n| Greenness (0–1) | Spots | Humidity (%) | Label |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| 0.88 | 0 | 45 | healthy |\r\n| 0.42 | 14 | 82 | sick |\r\n| 0.91 | 1 | 50 | healthy |\r\n| 0.55 | 9 | 76 | sick |\r\n\r\nThe first three columns are **features**. The last column is the **label**.\r\n\r\n### A Dataset in Python\r\n\r\nA list of dictionaries (Chapter 7) is a natural way to hold a dataset. We can split it into features and labels with a loop.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nleaves = [\r\n    {\"greenness\": 0.88, \"spots\": 0, \"label\": \"healthy\"},\r\n    {\"greenness\": 0.42, \"spots\": 14, \"label\": \"sick\"},\r\n    {\"greenness\": 0.91, \"spots\": 1, \"label\": \"healthy\"},\r\n    {\"greenness\": 0.55, \"spots\": 9, \"label\": \"sick\"}\r\n]\r\n\r\nfeatures = []\r\nlabels = []\r\nfor leaf in leaves:\r\n    features.append((leaf[\"greenness\"], leaf[\"spots\"]))\r\n    labels.append(leaf[\"label\"])\r\n\r\nprint(\"Examples:\", len(leaves))\r\nprint(\"Features (X):\", features)\r\nprint(\"Labels (y):\", labels)\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nExamples: 4\r\nFeatures (X): [(0.88, 0), (0.42, 14), (0.91, 1), (0.55, 9)]\r\nLabels (y): ['healthy', 'sick', 'healthy', 'sick']\r\n```\r\n\r\nEach feature pair is a tuple, because the measurements of one leaf belong together and should not change.\r\n\r\n## 🔬 Watching a Tiny Model Learn\r\n\r\nLet us see learning happen. A school records how many hours of sunshine each day had, and how much energy its solar panels made. This data is **made up** for teaching.\r\n\r\n| Sunshine (hours) | Energy (kWh) |\r\n| :--- | :--- |\r\n| 2 | 5 |\r\n| 4 | 11 |\r\n| 6 | 15 |\r\n| 8 | 21 |\r\n\r\nWe guess the model has the form **energy = w × hours**. The number `w` is called a **weight**. Learning means finding a good weight. Our method is simple:\r\n\r\n1. Try a weight.\r\n2. Predict the energy for every day in the data.\r\n3. Measure the **error**: how far each prediction is from the real value. Add up the sizes of the mistakes.\r\n4. Keep the weight with the smallest total error.\r\n\r\nThe built-in function `abs()` gives the **absolute value**: the size of a number without its minus sign. `abs(-3)` is `3`. We use it so that too-high and too-low guesses both count as mistakes.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nhours = [2, 4, 6, 8]\r\nenergy = [5, 11, 15, 21]\r\n\r\nweights_to_try = [1.0, 1.5, 2.0, 2.5, 3.0]\r\nbest_weight = 0\r\nbest_error = 1000\r\n\r\nfor w in weights_to_try:\r\n    total_error = 0\r\n    for i in range(len(hours)):\r\n        prediction = w * hours[i]\r\n        total_error += abs(prediction - energy[i])\r\n    print(f\"weight {w}: total error {total_error}\")\r\n    if total_error < best_error:\r\n        best_error = total_error\r\n        best_weight = w\r\n\r\nprint(f\"Best weight: {best_weight}\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nweight 1.0: total error 32.0\r\nweight 1.5: total error 22.0\r\nweight 2.0: total error 12.0\r\nweight 2.5: total error 2.0\r\nweight 3.0: total error 8.0\r\nBest weight: 2.5\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe weight `2.5` makes the smallest error, so the model is **energy = 2.5 × hours**. The computer found this by comparing its guesses with the real answers. That is the heart of machine learning: **guess, measure the error, improve**.\r\n\r\nNow we can use the model to make a **prediction** for a new day:\r\n\r\n```python\r\nbest_weight = 2.5\r\n\r\ndef predict_energy(sun_hours):\r\n    return best_weight * sun_hours\r\n\r\nprint(predict_energy(5))\r\nprint(predict_energy(7.5))\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\n12.5\r\n18.75\r\n```\r\n\r\n> 🧪 **Honest note**: this is a simplified teaching model. Real ML libraries try millions of tiny changes to many weights at once, using a branch of maths called calculus. But the idea is the same: reduce the error step by step.\r\n\r\n## 🌍 Real-World Connection\r\n\r\n| Helpful task | Features | Label |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| Spotting sick crop leaves | colour, spots, leaf size | healthy or sick |\r\n| Planning clean energy | sunshine, temperature | energy made (kWh) |\r\n| Water safety checks | acidity, cloudiness | safe or needs testing |\r\n| Library planning | month, school events | books borrowed |\r\n\r\n## ⚖️ What ML Cannot Do, and Why Ethics Matters\r\n\r\nA model only knows what its data showed it.\r\n\r\n- If the data is **biased** (unfair or one-sided) or incomplete, the model learns and repeats that bias.\r\n- A model can be **wrong**. A prediction is a best guess, not a fact. People must check important answers. In Islam, checking news before acting on it is called *Tabayyun*.\r\n- Personal data used for training must be collected honestly and kept safe (*Amānah*).\r\n- Technology stays under human care and moral judgement (*Iḥsān*).\r\n\r\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\r\n\r\n**Giving the model data in different units from its training data.** Our model learned from **hours**. Here someone gives it **minutes**:\r\n\r\n```python\r\ndef predict_energy(sun_hours):\r\n    return 2.5 * sun_hours\r\n\r\nsunshine_minutes = 300\r\nprint(predict_energy(sunshine_minutes))\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\n750.0\r\n```\r\n\r\n750 kWh is far too much for one school! The model is fine; the input is wrong. **Fixed version:** convert first, so the model sees the same kind of data it learned from.\r\n\r\n```python\r\ndef predict_energy(sun_hours):\r\n    return 2.5 * sun_hours\r\n\r\nsunshine_minutes = 300\r\nprint(predict_energy(sunshine_minutes / 60))\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\n12.5\r\n```\r\n\r\n## ✍️ Try It\r\n\r\nCopy the weight-learning program. Add `3.5` to `weights_to_try` and add a fifth day: 10 hours, 26 kWh. Run it again. Does the best weight change?\r\n\r\n**Hint:** among your list, the best weight should still be `2.5` (total error 3.0). Then add the in-between weight `2.6`. Its error is smaller (about 1.6), so trying more, finer weights can give a better model.\r\n\r\n## 📝 Recap\r\n\r\n- Normal programs follow rules people write. ML programs find the rules from examples.\r\n- Features are the inputs (X); the label is the answer (y); the model is the learned pattern.\r\n- Training means: guess, measure the error, and improve.\r\n- A model makes predictions for new data, but only works well on data like its training data.\r\n- Bias, mistakes and privacy are real risks, so people must check and care for AI systems.\r\n\r\n## 🏆 Practice\r\n\r\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\r\nIn a dataset about water wells, the columns are `depth_m`, `acidity`, `cloudiness` and `safe_to_drink`. Which columns are features and which is the label?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\nFeatures: `depth_m`, `acidity`, `cloudiness`. Label: `safe_to_drink` (the answer we want to predict).\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\r\nFor each task, name the features and the label.\r\n\r\n1. A weather station predicts whether it will rain tomorrow from air pressure, wind speed and cloud cover.\r\n2. A school estimates how many litres of water its garden needs from the temperature and the number of plants.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. Features: air pressure, wind speed, cloud cover. Label: rain (yes or no).\r\n2. Features: temperature, number of plants. Label: litres of water.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\r\nA made-up dataset links the number of students in a class to the litres of drinking water they use in a day: `students = [10, 20, 30]`, `litres = [16, 30, 47]`.\r\n\r\n1. Use the model **litres = w × students**.\r\n2. Try the weights `1.0, 1.5, 2.0`.\r\n3. Print the total error for each weight and the best weight.\r\n4. Predict the litres for a class of 25 students.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```python\r\nstudents = [10, 20, 30]\r\nlitres = [16, 30, 47]\r\n\r\nbest_weight = 0\r\nbest_error = 1000\r\nfor w in [1.0, 1.5, 2.0]:\r\n    total_error = 0\r\n    for i in range(len(students)):\r\n        total_error += abs(w * students[i] - litres[i])\r\n    print(f\"weight {w}: total error {total_error}\")\r\n    if total_error < best_error:\r\n        best_error = total_error\r\n        best_weight = w\r\n\r\nprint(\"Best weight:\", best_weight)\r\nprint(\"Prediction for 25 students:\", best_weight * 25)\r\n```\r\n\r\nOutput:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nweight 1.0: total error 33.0\r\nweight 1.5: total error 3.0\r\nweight 2.0: total error 27.0\r\nBest weight: 1.5\r\nPrediction for 25 students: 37.5\r\n```\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n",
  "part-05-machine-learning-foundations/ch12-supervised-vs-unsupervised.md": "# Chapter 12: Supervised vs. Unsupervised Learning 🎯\r\n\r\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\r\n\r\nImagine two ways of learning about plants. In the first, your biology teacher shows you leaves and says the name of each one. You practise, and she corrects your mistakes. In the second, you find a box of seeds with no labels at all. Nobody tells you their names, but you can still sort them into piles: small and round, long and thin, large and flat. Machine learning has the same two styles. Learning **with** answers is **supervised**. Finding groups **without** answers is **unsupervised**.\r\n\r\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\r\n\r\n- Explain supervised learning and its two kinds: classification and regression.\r\n- Build a simple nearest-neighbour classifier in plain Python.\r\n- Explain unsupervised learning and what clustering means.\r\n- Group numbers into clusters with a small, step-by-step version of k-means.\r\n- Choose the right kind of learning for a task.\r\n\r\n## 🎓 Supervised Learning: Learning with a Teacher\r\n\r\nIn **supervised learning**, every training example has **features** and the correct **label**. The label acts like a teacher. The algorithm makes a guess, compares it with the label and adjusts itself.\r\n\r\nThink of flashcards. The front of the card shows the features (a picture of a fruit). The back shows the label (\"mango\").\r\n\r\nSupervised learning has two main kinds:\r\n\r\n| Kind | The answer is… | Example |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| **Classification** | a category (a group name) | Is this crop leaf healthy or sick? |\r\n| **Regression** | a number on a scale | How many kWh will the solar panels make? |\r\n\r\nThe sunshine model in Chapter 11 was **regression**: it predicted a number. Now let us build a **classifier**.\r\n\r\n## 🥭 A Nearest-Neighbour Classifier\r\n\r\nA fruit packing group sorts mangoes into \"ripe\" and \"unripe\". For each mango, they measure two features on a 1 to 10 scale: **softness** and **colour** (1 = green, 10 = deep yellow). Here is a small, **made-up** training set:\r\n\r\n| Softness | Colour | Label |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| 2 | 3 | unripe |\r\n| 3 | 2 | unripe |\r\n| 1 | 4 | unripe |\r\n| 7 | 8 | ripe |\r\n| 8 | 7 | ripe |\r\n| 9 | 9 | ripe |\r\n\r\nThe **nearest-neighbour** method is simple: to label a new mango, find the training mango that is **most similar** to it, and copy its label.\r\n\r\n\"Most similar\" means the smallest **distance**. Imagine each mango as a dot on a grid, with softness across and colour up. The distance between two dots is measured with Pythagoras' theorem: the square root of (difference across)² + (difference up)².\r\n\r\n```\r\ncolour\r\n 10│              ● ripe\r\n  8│           ●  ●\r\n  6│        ★ new mango?\r\n  4│ ○\r\n  2│    ○  ○ unripe\r\n   └─────────────────── softness\r\n     2    4    6    8\r\n```\r\n\r\n```python\r\nimport math\r\n\r\ntraining = [\r\n    ((2, 3), \"unripe\"),\r\n    ((3, 2), \"unripe\"),\r\n    ((1, 4), \"unripe\"),\r\n    ((7, 8), \"ripe\"),\r\n    ((8, 7), \"ripe\"),\r\n    ((9, 9), \"ripe\")\r\n]\r\n\r\ndef distance(a, b):\r\n    \"\"\"Straight-line distance between two (softness, colour) points.\"\"\"\r\n    return math.sqrt((a[0] - b[0]) ** 2 + (a[1] - b[1]) ** 2)\r\n\r\ndef classify(new_mango):\r\n    best_label = \"\"\r\n    best_distance = 1000\r\n    for features, label in training:\r\n        d = distance(new_mango, features)\r\n        if d < best_distance:\r\n            best_distance = d\r\n            best_label = label\r\n    return best_label, round(best_distance, 2)\r\n\r\nprint(classify((6, 6)))\r\nprint(classify((2, 2)))\r\nprint(classify((5, 4)))\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\n('ripe', 2.24)\r\n('unripe', 1.0)\r\n('unripe', 2.83)\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe classifier never received a rule like \"softness above 5 means ripe\". It **learned from the examples**: whatever the new mango is closest to decides the answer. The third mango (5, 4) sits between the two groups. Its nearest neighbour is the unripe mango (3, 2), at 2.83. The closest ripe mango is 4.24 away. Cases in the middle like this are worth checking by hand.\r\n\r\n> 🧪 **Honest note**: this is a simplified teaching model with six made-up mangoes. Real classifiers use far more examples, and often look at several neighbours (the \"k\" in **k-nearest neighbours**) and let them vote.\r\n\r\n## 🔍 Unsupervised Learning: Finding Groups Alone\r\n\r\nIn **unsupervised learning** there are **no labels**. We give the computer raw data and ask: *\"What natural groups are hiding in here?\"* Finding groups of similar items is called **clustering**.\r\n\r\n```\r\nBefore (no labels):    After clustering:\r\n  •   •      •          A A      B\r\n •  •      •  •        A A     B  B\r\n   •         •           A       B\r\n```\r\n\r\nFor example, a relief agency might have the locations of the villages it serves, but no plan for where to build supply hubs. A clustering method called **k-means** can find **k** groups of nearby villages. One hub can then be placed in the middle of each group.\r\n\r\n### Clustering Step by Step\r\n\r\nA town has 8 public water taps. The council records the litres each tap gives out per day. The numbers are **made up**. Is there a \"busy\" group and a \"quiet\" group? We do not have labels; we let the data decide.\r\n\r\nThe k-means idea for two groups:\r\n\r\n1. Pick two starting **centres** (here: the smallest and largest values).\r\n2. Put every value in the group of its **nearest** centre.\r\n3. Move each centre to the **average** of its group.\r\n4. Repeat steps 2 and 3 a few times until the centres stop moving.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nlitres = [120, 135, 410, 128, 395, 140, 420, 405]\r\n\r\ncentre_a = min(litres)\r\ncentre_b = max(litres)\r\n\r\nfor round_number in range(1, 4):\r\n    group_a = []\r\n    group_b = []\r\n    for value in litres:\r\n        if abs(value - centre_a) <= abs(value - centre_b):\r\n            group_a.append(value)\r\n        else:\r\n            group_b.append(value)\r\n    centre_a = sum(group_a) / len(group_a)\r\n    centre_b = sum(group_b) / len(group_b)\r\n    print(f\"Round {round_number}: centres {round(centre_a, 1)} and {round(centre_b, 1)}\")\r\n\r\nprint(\"Group A:\", group_a)\r\nprint(\"Group B:\", group_b)\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nRound 1: centres 130.8 and 407.5\r\nRound 2: centres 130.8 and 407.5\r\nRound 3: centres 130.8 and 407.5\r\nGroup A: [120, 135, 128, 140]\r\nGroup B: [410, 395, 420, 405]\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe centres settle after the first round. The computer found two clusters by itself: taps that give about 131 litres a day and taps that give about 408. It does **not** know *why*. Perhaps the busy taps are near the market. Explaining the groups is the human's job.\r\n\r\n## 🧭 Supervised or Unsupervised?\r\n\r\n| | Supervised | Unsupervised |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| Labels in the data? | Yes | No |\r\n| Question | \"What is the answer for this new example?\" | \"What groups are in this data?\" |\r\n| Kinds | Classification, regression | Clustering (and more) |\r\n| Example | Ripe or unripe mango | Busy and quiet water taps |\r\n| Checking | Compare with known labels | People judge if the groups make sense |\r\n\r\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\r\n\r\n**Forgetting `abs()` when measuring a distance.** A distance can never be negative. Without `abs()`, a value far **below** looks like the \"closest\".\r\n\r\nHere we classify a mango by softness alone. The new mango has softness 6.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nexamples = [(2, \"unripe\"), (7, \"ripe\")]\r\nnew_softness = 6\r\n\r\nbest_label = \"\"\r\nbest_distance = 1000\r\nfor softness, label in examples:\r\n    d = softness - new_softness          # wrong: can be negative\r\n    if d < best_distance:\r\n        best_distance = d\r\n        best_label = label\r\nprint(best_label, best_distance)\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nunripe -4\r\n```\r\n\r\nA distance of −4 is nonsense, and softness 6 is clearly nearer to 7. **Fixed version:** use `abs()`.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nexamples = [(2, \"unripe\"), (7, \"ripe\")]\r\nnew_softness = 6\r\n\r\nbest_label = \"\"\r\nbest_distance = 1000\r\nfor softness, label in examples:\r\n    d = abs(softness - new_softness)     # right: always 0 or more\r\n    if d < best_distance:\r\n        best_distance = d\r\n        best_label = label\r\nprint(best_label, best_distance)\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nripe 1\r\n```\r\n\r\n## ✍️ Try It\r\n\r\nAdd two more mangoes to the `training` list: `((5, 6), \"ripe\")` and `((4, 3), \"unripe\")`. Run `classify((5, 4))` again. Does the answer or the distance change?\r\n\r\n**Hint:** work out the distance from (5, 4) to (4, 3) by hand: √(1² + 1²) ≈ 1.41.\r\n\r\n## 📝 Recap\r\n\r\n- Supervised learning uses labelled examples. Classification predicts a category; regression predicts a number.\r\n- A nearest-neighbour classifier copies the label of the most similar training example.\r\n- Unsupervised learning has no labels. Clustering finds groups of similar items.\r\n- K-means repeats two steps: put each item with its nearest centre, then move each centre to its group's average.\r\n- The computer finds groups; people must explain them and check they make sense.\r\n\r\n## 🏆 Practice\r\n\r\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\r\nIs each task classification, regression or clustering?\r\n\r\n1. Predicting the millimetres of rain next Wednesday.\r\n2. Deciding if a water sample is \"safe\" or \"needs a lab test\".\r\n3. Grouping library books into topics when none of them have subject labels.\r\n4. Estimating how many minutes an ambulance needs to reach the hospital.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n1. Regression (a number).\r\n2. Classification (a category).\r\n3. Clustering (unsupervised: no labels).\r\n4. Regression (a number of minutes).\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\r\nUsing the `distance()` function from this chapter, calculate the distance between the points (1, 1) and (4, 5).\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```python\r\nimport math\r\n\r\ndef distance(a, b):\r\n    return math.sqrt((a[0] - b[0]) ** 2 + (a[1] - b[1]) ** 2)\r\n\r\nprint(distance((1, 1), (4, 5)))\r\n```\r\n\r\nOutput:\r\n\r\n```text\r\n5.0\r\n```\r\n\r\nAcross 3, up 4: √(9 + 16) = √25 = 5.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\r\nA charity weighed the parcels waiting to be delivered, in kilograms (made-up data): `[3, 4, 21, 2, 19, 5, 20]`.\r\n\r\n1. Use the k-means steps from this chapter with two centres, starting at the smallest and largest values.\r\n2. Run 3 rounds and print the centres each round.\r\n3. Print both groups.\r\n4. In one sentence, explain why a volunteer, not the computer, should decide what the groups mean.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```python\r\nparcels = [3, 4, 21, 2, 19, 5, 20]\r\ncentre_a = min(parcels)\r\ncentre_b = max(parcels)\r\n\r\nfor r in range(1, 4):\r\n    group_a = []\r\n    group_b = []\r\n    for m in parcels:\r\n        if abs(m - centre_a) <= abs(m - centre_b):\r\n            group_a.append(m)\r\n        else:\r\n            group_b.append(m)\r\n    centre_a = sum(group_a) / len(group_a)\r\n    centre_b = sum(group_b) / len(group_b)\r\n    print(f\"Round {r}: {round(centre_a, 1)} and {round(centre_b, 1)}\")\r\n\r\nprint(\"Group A:\", group_a)\r\nprint(\"Group B:\", group_b)\r\n```\r\n\r\nOutput:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nRound 1: 3.5 and 20.0\r\nRound 2: 3.5 and 20.0\r\nRound 3: 3.5 and 20.0\r\nGroup A: [3, 4, 2, 5]\r\nGroup B: [21, 19, 20]\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe computer only sees numbers. A volunteer knows what is inside: perhaps the light parcels are food boxes and the heavy ones are blankets, which need a van, not a bicycle.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n",
  "part-05-machine-learning-foundations/ch13-the-ml-pipeline-and-evaluation.md": "# Chapter 13: The Machine Learning Pipeline & Evaluation 📈\r\n\r\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\r\n\r\nBefore a maths exam, Ahmed found last year's paper and learned every answer by heart. In the practice test he scored 100%. In the real exam, the numbers were different, and he was lost. He had **memorised**, not **learned**. Machine learning models can make the same mistake. In this chapter you will see how engineers build a model step by step, and how they test it honestly so that a \"perfect score\" is not a trick.\r\n\r\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\r\n\r\n- List the five steps of the machine learning pipeline.\r\n- Clean a small dataset: remove missing values and duplicates.\r\n- Split data into a training set and a test set, and explain why.\r\n- Train a simple threshold model and measure its accuracy on new data.\r\n- Count false positives and false negatives, and explain why accuracy alone can mislead.\r\n\r\n## 🔄 The Five Steps of the ML Pipeline\r\n\r\nA **pipeline** is a fixed series of steps where the output of one step feeds the next.\r\n\r\n```\r\n1. Collect   gather honest, fair data\r\n     │\r\n2. Clean     fix gaps, duplicates, typos\r\n     │\r\n3. Split     training set + test set\r\n     │\r\n4. Train     the model learns from\r\n     │       the training set only\r\n5. Evaluate  test on unseen data,\r\n             check for unfair errors\r\n```\r\n\r\n## 🧹 Steps 1 and 2: Collect and Clean\r\n\r\nReal data is messy. Sensors fail, people type values twice, and labels are spelled in different ways. If messy data goes in, a messy model comes out. Engineers call this \"garbage in, garbage out\".\r\n\r\nHere is a small, **made-up** set of water samples. Each sample has a **cloudiness** score from 0 (crystal clear) to 10 (very muddy), and a label from a laboratory test. Some entries have problems.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nraw_samples = [\r\n    (1, \"clean\"), (7, \"Polluted\"), (2, \"clean\"), (None, \"clean\"),\r\n    (8, \"polluted\"), (3, \"clean\"), (6, \"polluted\"), (8, \"polluted\"),\r\n    (0, \"clean\"), (9, \"polluted\"), (4, \"clean\"), (5, \"polluted\")\r\n]\r\n\r\nsamples = []\r\nfor cloudiness, label in raw_samples:\r\n    if cloudiness is None:\r\n        continue                      # missing value: skip\r\n    label = label.lower()             # fix \"Polluted\" -> \"polluted\"\r\n    if (cloudiness, label) in samples:\r\n        continue                      # duplicate: skip\r\n    samples.append((cloudiness, label))\r\n\r\nprint(\"Before cleaning:\", len(raw_samples))\r\nprint(\"After cleaning: \", len(samples))\r\nprint(samples)\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nBefore cleaning: 12\r\nAfter cleaning:  10\r\n[(1, 'clean'), (7, 'polluted'), (2, 'clean'), (8, 'polluted'), (3, 'clean'), (6, 'polluted'), (0, 'clean'), (9, 'polluted'), (4, 'clean'), (5, 'polluted')]\r\n```\r\n\r\n- `is None` checks for a missing value. `None` means \"no value was recorded\".\r\n- `.lower()` makes the labels consistent.\r\n- The second `(8, \"polluted\")` was a duplicate, so we kept only one.\r\n\r\n(Real projects do not always delete duplicates. Two different samples can have the same reading. Here we pretend the second one was typed twice by mistake.)\r\n\r\n## ✂️ Step 3: Split into Training and Test Sets\r\n\r\nWe never test a model on the same examples it learned from. That would be like Ahmed's practice test. Instead, we **split** the data:\r\n\r\n- **Training set** (about 80%): the model learns from these.\r\n- **Test set** (about 20%): locked away until training is finished. Then it is used as an honest exam.\r\n\r\nOur cleaned data has 10 samples, so 8 go to training and 2 to testing. Slicing (Chapter 6) does this neatly. (Real projects first **shuffle** the data into random order, so the test set is a fair mix.)\r\n\r\n```python\r\nsamples = [(1, \"clean\"), (7, \"polluted\"), (2, \"clean\"), (8, \"polluted\"),\r\n           (3, \"clean\"), (6, \"polluted\"), (0, \"clean\"), (9, \"polluted\"),\r\n           (4, \"clean\"), (5, \"polluted\")]\r\n\r\ncut = int(len(samples) * 0.8)\r\ntrain = samples[:cut]\r\ntest = samples[cut:]\r\n\r\nprint(\"Training:\", len(train), train)\r\nprint(\"Test:    \", len(test), test)\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nTraining: 8 [(1, 'clean'), (7, 'polluted'), (2, 'clean'), (8, 'polluted'), (3, 'clean'), (6, 'polluted'), (0, 'clean'), (9, 'polluted')]\r\nTest:     2 [(4, 'clean'), (5, 'polluted')]\r\n```\r\n\r\n## 🏋️ Step 4: Train a Threshold Model\r\n\r\nOur model will be a single rule: **\"if cloudiness is at least T, predict polluted\"**. The number **T** is the **threshold**. Training means finding the best T, using the training set only. We try every T from 1 to 9 and count the mistakes.\r\n\r\n```python\r\ntrain = [(1, \"clean\"), (7, \"polluted\"), (2, \"clean\"), (8, \"polluted\"),\r\n         (3, \"clean\"), (6, \"polluted\"), (0, \"clean\"), (9, \"polluted\")]\r\n\r\ndef predict(cloudiness, threshold):\r\n    if cloudiness >= threshold:\r\n        return \"polluted\"\r\n    return \"clean\"\r\n\r\nbest_t = 0\r\nfewest_mistakes = 1000\r\nfor t in range(1, 10):\r\n    mistakes = 0\r\n    for cloudiness, label in train:\r\n        if predict(cloudiness, t) != label:\r\n            mistakes += 1\r\n    print(f\"T = {t}: {mistakes} mistake(s)\")\r\n    if mistakes < fewest_mistakes:\r\n        fewest_mistakes = mistakes\r\n        best_t = t\r\n\r\nprint(\"Chosen threshold:\", best_t)\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nT = 1: 3 mistake(s)\r\nT = 2: 2 mistake(s)\r\nT = 3: 1 mistake(s)\r\nT = 4: 0 mistake(s)\r\nT = 5: 0 mistake(s)\r\nT = 6: 0 mistake(s)\r\nT = 7: 1 mistake(s)\r\nT = 8: 2 mistake(s)\r\nT = 9: 3 mistake(s)\r\nChosen threshold: 4\r\n```\r\n\r\nThresholds 4, 5 and 6 all make zero mistakes on the training data. Our loop keeps the **first** best one, so it chooses T = 4.\r\n\r\n## 🧪 Step 5: Evaluate on the Test Set\r\n\r\nNow we unlock the test set. **Accuracy** is the share of correct predictions:\r\n\r\n**Accuracy = correct predictions ÷ total predictions × 100**\r\n\r\n```python\r\ntest = [(4, \"clean\"), (5, \"polluted\")]\r\nbest_t = 4\r\n\r\ndef predict(cloudiness, threshold):\r\n    if cloudiness >= threshold:\r\n        return \"polluted\"\r\n    return \"clean\"\r\n\r\ncorrect = 0\r\nfor cloudiness, label in test:\r\n    guess = predict(cloudiness, best_t)\r\n    print(f\"cloudiness {cloudiness}: predicted {guess}, actual {label}\")\r\n    if guess == label:\r\n        correct += 1\r\n\r\naccuracy = correct / len(test) * 100\r\nprint(f\"Test accuracy: {accuracy}%\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\ncloudiness 4: predicted polluted, actual clean\r\ncloudiness 5: predicted polluted, actual polluted\r\nTest accuracy: 50.0%\r\n```\r\n\r\nTraining looked perfect, but the test found a mistake. The training data had no samples between 3 and 6, so the model could not know where the real border was. The honest fix is **more data near the border**, not hiding the result. With only 2 test samples, one mistake swings accuracy to 50%, which is another reason real tests use many more samples.\r\n\r\n> 🧪 **Honest note**: this is a simplified teaching model with ten made-up samples. Real water testing is done in a laboratory. A cloudiness score alone can never prove that water is safe to drink.\r\n\r\n## 🧠 Overfitting: Memorising Instead of Learning\r\n\r\nA model that does brilliantly on its training data but badly on new data is **overfitting**. The most extreme case is a \"model\" that simply memorises every training answer:\r\n\r\n```python\r\ntrain = [(1, \"clean\"), (7, \"polluted\"), (2, \"clean\"), (8, \"polluted\")]\r\n\r\nmemory = {}\r\nfor cloudiness, label in train:\r\n    memory[cloudiness] = label\r\n\r\nprint(\"Sample 7:\", memory.get(7, \"no idea\"))\r\nprint(\"Sample 2:\", memory.get(2, \"no idea\"))\r\nprint(\"New sample 6:\", memory.get(6, \"no idea\"))\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nSample 7: polluted\r\nSample 2: clean\r\nNew sample 6: no idea\r\n```\r\n\r\nIt scores 100% on what it has seen and is useless on anything new. A good model learns a **general pattern**, like our threshold rule.\r\n\r\n## ⚖️ Accuracy Is Not Everything\r\n\r\nTwo kinds of mistakes can happen when the model looks for pollution:\r\n\r\n| | Model says polluted | Model says clean |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| **Really polluted** | True positive (TP) ✅ | **False negative (FN)** ❌ dangerous |\r\n| **Really clean** | **False positive (FP)** ❌ extra test | True negative (TN) ✅ |\r\n\r\n- A **false positive** flags clean water as polluted. The cost: an extra lab test.\r\n- A **false negative** misses real pollution. The cost: people may drink unsafe water.\r\n\r\nNow look at a lazy model that **always** says \"clean\":\r\n\r\n```python\r\nactual = [\"clean\", \"clean\", \"polluted\", \"clean\", \"clean\",\r\n          \"clean\", \"polluted\", \"clean\", \"clean\", \"clean\"]\r\n\r\ntp = 0\r\ntn = 0\r\nfp = 0\r\nfn = 0\r\nfor i in range(len(actual)):\r\n    guess = \"clean\"                    # the lazy model\r\n    if actual[i] == \"polluted\" and guess == \"polluted\":\r\n        tp += 1\r\n    elif actual[i] == \"clean\" and guess == \"clean\":\r\n        tn += 1\r\n    elif actual[i] == \"clean\" and guess == \"polluted\":\r\n        fp += 1\r\n    else:\r\n        fn += 1\r\n\r\naccuracy = (tp + tn) / len(actual) * 100\r\nprint(f\"Accuracy: {accuracy}%\")\r\nprint(f\"False positives: {fp}\")\r\nprint(f\"False negatives: {fn}  <- missed pollution!\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nAccuracy: 80.0%\r\nFalse positives: 0\r\nFalse negatives: 2  <- missed pollution!\r\n```\r\n\r\n80% accuracy sounds good, yet this model missed **every** polluted sample. When one kind of mistake can hurt people, engineers count each kind separately. Caring about who could be harmed is part of *Iḥsān*.\r\n\r\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\r\n\r\n**Testing the model on its own training data.** The score looks perfect, but it proves nothing about new data.\r\n\r\n```python\r\ntrain = [(1, \"clean\"), (7, \"polluted\"), (2, \"clean\"), (8, \"polluted\"),\r\n         (3, \"clean\"), (6, \"polluted\"), (0, \"clean\"), (9, \"polluted\")]\r\nbest_t = 4\r\n\r\ncorrect = 0\r\nfor cloudiness, label in train:          # wrong: these were used for training\r\n    guess = \"clean\"\r\n    if cloudiness >= best_t:\r\n        guess = \"polluted\"\r\n    if guess == label:\r\n        correct += 1\r\nprint(f\"Accuracy: {correct / len(train) * 100}%\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nAccuracy: 100.0%\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Fixed version:** loop over the `test` set instead. It gave 50% above: a less pleasing number, but the honest one.\r\n\r\n## ✍️ Try It\r\n\r\nChange the training loop so it tries thresholds from 9 **down** to 1 (`range(9, 0, -1)`). Which threshold is chosen now? Test it on `[(4, \"clean\"), (5, \"polluted\")]`. What is the new accuracy?\r\n\r\n**Hint:** the first threshold with zero mistakes is now 6. Then cloudiness 5 is predicted \"clean\", which is wrong.\r\n\r\n## 📝 Recap\r\n\r\n- The pipeline is: collect, clean, split, train, evaluate.\r\n- Clean data by removing missing values and duplicates and making labels consistent.\r\n- Train on the training set only. Keep the test set hidden until the end.\r\n- Accuracy = correct ÷ total × 100. A perfect training score may just mean overfitting.\r\n- Count false positives and false negatives separately, especially when a missed case can harm people.\r\n\r\n## 🏆 Practice\r\n\r\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\r\nWhat is the difference between a false positive and a false negative in a pollution detector? Which one is usually more dangerous?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\nA **false positive** says \"polluted\" when the water is clean. A **false negative** says \"clean\" when the water is polluted. The false negative is more dangerous, because pollution is missed.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\r\nA dataset has 250 examples. Using an 80/20 split, how many examples go in each set? Write the Python line that finds the cut point.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n200 for training and 50 for testing.\r\n\r\n```python\r\ncut = int(250 * 0.8)\r\nprint(cut)\r\n```\r\n\r\nOutput:\r\n\r\n```text\r\n200\r\n```\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\r\nA water sensor model was tested on 8 samples:\r\n\r\n```python\r\nactual    = [\"clean\", \"clean\", \"polluted\", \"clean\", \"polluted\", \"polluted\", \"clean\", \"clean\"]\r\npredicted = [\"clean\", \"clean\", \"clean\", \"clean\", \"polluted\", \"polluted\", \"polluted\", \"clean\"]\r\n```\r\n\r\n1. Loop with `for i in range(len(actual)):`.\r\n2. Count TP, TN, FP and FN (where \"polluted\" is the positive class).\r\n3. Print the accuracy and the FP and FN counts.\r\n4. Say in one sentence which mistake you would fix first, and why.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```python\r\ntp = 0\r\ntn = 0\r\nfp = 0\r\nfn = 0\r\nfor i in range(len(actual)):\r\n    a = actual[i]\r\n    p = predicted[i]\r\n    if a == \"polluted\" and p == \"polluted\":\r\n        tp += 1\r\n    elif a == \"clean\" and p == \"clean\":\r\n        tn += 1\r\n    elif a == \"clean\" and p == \"polluted\":\r\n        fp += 1\r\n    else:\r\n        fn += 1\r\n\r\nprint(f\"Accuracy: {(tp + tn) / len(actual) * 100}%\")\r\nprint(f\"False positives: {fp}\")\r\nprint(f\"False negatives: {fn}\")\r\n```\r\n\r\nOutput:\r\n\r\n```text\r\nAccuracy: 75.0%\r\nFalse positives: 1\r\nFalse negatives: 1\r\n```\r\n\r\nFix the false negative first: it let polluted water be called clean, which could make people ill.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n",
  "part-06-ethical-ai-and-capstones/ch14-ai-ethics-and-amanah.md": "# Chapter 14: AI Ethics, Transparency & Digital Trust (*Amānah*) 🛡️\r\n\r\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\r\n\r\nWhen a neighbour leaves her house key with your family before a journey, she trusts you. You would never open her cupboards or show her letters to anyone. In Arabic, this kind of trust is called *Amānah*. Data is like that key. When people share their information with a program, they hand its makers a trust. AI is powerful, and in this chapter you will learn to use that power with honesty, fairness and care.\r\n\r\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\r\n\r\n- Explain five principles of ethical AI, linked to *Amānah*, *Ṣidq* (truthfulness) and *Iḥsān* (excellence).\r\n- Protect privacy by collecting less data and removing names before analysis.\r\n- Audit a dataset for under-represented groups, and check a model's accuracy for each group.\r\n- Make a model explain its decisions.\r\n- Practise *Tabayyun*: checking an AI answer before trusting it.\r\n\r\n## 🏛️ Five Principles of Ethical AI\r\n\r\n### 🔐 Privacy as a Trust (*Amānah*)\r\n\r\nEvery row of data stands for a real person: their family, their health, their home. So:\r\n\r\n- Collect **only** the data you really need. This is called **data minimisation**.\r\n- Ask for honest, clear permission (**consent**) before collecting personal data.\r\n- Never store passwords as plain text, and keep personal records locked away.\r\n\r\nBetraying someone's data is a betrayal of trust (*Khiyānah*).\r\n\r\n### ⚖️ Fairness and Bias\r\n\r\nA model learns from its data, including the data's gaps. For example:\r\n\r\n- A plant-disease model trained only on photos taken in bright midday sun may fail on cloudy, rainy farms.\r\n- A system trained on old records where some groups were left out will repeat that unfairness.\r\n\r\nEthical engineers test their models across different groups and conditions **before** anyone relies on them.\r\n\r\n### 🗣️ Truthfulness (*Ṣidq*)\r\n\r\n- **Deepfakes** are fake videos or voices made by AI to show people saying things they never said. Making them to deceive is falsehood and slander (*Buhtān*).\r\n- **Dark patterns** are designs that trick users, for example into endless scrolling. We do not build them.\r\n- AI answers can be confidently **wrong**. Before you share or act on one, check it. This is *Tabayyun*: verifying news before acting on it.\r\n\r\n### 🔍 Explainability\r\n\r\nIf an AI system affects someone, such as the order of a clinic queue or a scholarship decision, that person has a right to know **why**. Prefer models whose reasons can be shown, like the threshold rule in Chapter 13, over \"black boxes\" that nobody can explain.\r\n\r\n### 🚫 Things We Refuse to Build\r\n\r\nAs ethical technologists, we do not build:\r\n\r\n- Interest-based loan calculators (*Ribā*) or games-of-chance predictors (*Maysir*).\r\n- Surveillance that tracks innocent people, or face recognition used to watch them.\r\n- Systems that guess private things about people, such as their beliefs or health, without consent.\r\n\r\n## 🧽 Privacy in Code: Remove What You Do Not Need\r\n\r\nA study club wants to know which subjects need more tutoring. The sign-up sheet asked for too much. Before analysing, we keep only what the question needs, and replace names with codes. Removing names like this is called **anonymising** data.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nsignups = [\r\n    {\"name\": \"Amina Yusuf\", \"phone\": \"0700-111\", \"subject\": \"maths\"},\r\n    {\"name\": \"Bilal Khan\", \"phone\": \"0700-222\", \"subject\": \"physics\"},\r\n    {\"name\": \"Hana Ali\", \"phone\": \"0700-333\", \"subject\": \"maths\"}\r\n]\r\n\r\nsafe_rows = []\r\nnumber = 1\r\nfor person in signups:\r\n    safe_rows.append({\"student\": f\"S{number}\", \"subject\": person[\"subject\"]})\r\n    number += 1\r\n\r\nprint(safe_rows)\r\n\r\ncounts = {}\r\nfor row in safe_rows:\r\n    counts[row[\"subject\"]] = counts.get(row[\"subject\"], 0) + 1\r\nprint(counts)\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\n[{'student': 'S1', 'subject': 'maths'}, {'student': 'S2', 'subject': 'physics'}, {'student': 'S3', 'subject': 'maths'}]\r\n{'maths': 2, 'physics': 1}\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe answer (\"maths needs more tutors\") did not need any names or phone numbers. The safest data is the data you never keep.\r\n\r\n## 📊 Auditing a Dataset for Representation\r\n\r\nBefore training, a careful data scientist checks: does the dataset include everyone the model will serve? Here is a **made-up** clinic study sample.\r\n\r\n```python\r\npatients = [\r\n    {\"region\": \"Rural North\", \"age_group\": \"Elderly\"},\r\n    {\"region\": \"Urban Centre\", \"age_group\": \"Young Adult\"},\r\n    {\"region\": \"Urban Centre\", \"age_group\": \"Adult\"},\r\n    {\"region\": \"Urban Centre\", \"age_group\": \"Young Adult\"},\r\n    {\"region\": \"Urban Centre\", \"age_group\": \"Adult\"},\r\n    {\"region\": \"Rural North\", \"age_group\": \"Adult\"}\r\n]\r\n\r\nregion_counts = {}\r\nfor p in patients:\r\n    region = p[\"region\"]\r\n    region_counts[region] = region_counts.get(region, 0) + 1\r\n\r\ntotal = len(patients)\r\nprint(\"DATASET AUDIT\")\r\nfor region, count in region_counts.items():\r\n    share = count / total * 100\r\n    print(f\"{region}: {count} ({round(share, 1)}%)\")\r\n\r\nrural_share = region_counts.get(\"Rural North\", 0) / total\r\nif rural_share < 0.40:\r\n    print(\"Warning: rural patients are under-represented.\")\r\n    print(\"Action: collect more rural samples before training.\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nDATASET AUDIT\r\nRural North: 2 (33.3%)\r\nUrban Centre: 4 (66.7%)\r\nWarning: rural patients are under-represented.\r\nAction: collect more rural samples before training.\r\n```\r\n\r\nIf half the people the clinic serves live in rural areas, a sample that is only one-third rural is not fair. The model would learn mostly about city patients.\r\n\r\n### Checking Accuracy for Each Group\r\n\r\nA model can have good **overall** accuracy and still fail one group. So we measure accuracy **per group**. These test results are made up:\r\n\r\n```python\r\nresults = [\r\n    (\"city\", True), (\"city\", True), (\"city\", True), (\"city\", False),\r\n    (\"city\", True), (\"city\", True), (\"rural\", False), (\"rural\", True),\r\n    (\"rural\", False), (\"rural\", True)\r\n]\r\n\r\ncorrect = {}\r\nseen = {}\r\nfor group, was_correct in results:\r\n    seen[group] = seen.get(group, 0) + 1\r\n    if was_correct:\r\n        correct[group] = correct.get(group, 0) + 1\r\n\r\noverall = 0\r\nfor group in seen:\r\n    overall += correct.get(group, 0)\r\nprint(f\"Overall accuracy: {overall / len(results) * 100}%\")\r\n\r\nfor group in seen:\r\n    accuracy = correct.get(group, 0) / seen[group] * 100\r\n    print(f\"{group}: {round(accuracy, 1)}% correct\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nOverall accuracy: 70.0%\r\ncity: 83.3% correct\r\nrural: 50.0% correct\r\n```\r\n\r\n70% overall hides a real problem: the model is right only half the time for rural cases. Fixing it might mean collecting better rural data. Treating every community fairly is *'Adl* (justice).\r\n\r\n## 💬 A Model That Explains Itself\r\n\r\nA transparent model shows the reasons for each decision. This simple, **made-up** rule decides whether a school garden bed needs attention, and lists every reason it used.\r\n\r\n```python\r\ndef check_bed(bed):\r\n    reasons = []\r\n    if bed[\"moisture\"] < 30:\r\n        reasons.append(\"soil is dry\")\r\n    if bed[\"yellow_leaves\"] > 5:\r\n        reasons.append(\"many yellow leaves\")\r\n    if len(reasons) > 0:\r\n        return \"needs attention\", reasons\r\n    return \"fine\", reasons\r\n\r\ndecision, reasons = check_bed({\"moisture\": 22, \"yellow_leaves\": 8})\r\nprint(\"Decision:\", decision)\r\nprint(\"Because:\", reasons)\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nDecision: needs attention\r\nBecause: ['soil is dry', 'many yellow leaves']\r\n```\r\n\r\nA gardener can read the reasons, disagree with them, and correct the rule. That is the kind of system people can trust.\r\n\r\n## 🔎 *Tabayyun*: Checking AI Answers\r\n\r\n| An AI tool tells you… | How to check it |\r\n| :--- | :--- |\r\n| A fact for your homework | Find it in your textbook or a trusted source |\r\n| A piece of code | Run it and test it with your own examples |\r\n| A quote from a person | Find the original speech or book |\r\n| A medical tip | Ask a doctor or nurse; never rely on AI alone |\r\n\r\n## 🤲 The Ethical Technologist's Oath\r\n\r\nBefore releasing any program into the world, ask yourself:\r\n\r\n1. Does it bring real benefit (*Naf'*) to people and protect their well-being?\r\n2. Would I be proud if my teachers, parents and community saw how it works inside?\r\n3. Am I handling people's data as a sacred trust (*Amānah*)?\r\n4. Have I checked that it treats every group fairly (*'Adl*)?\r\n\r\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\r\n\r\n**Showing more personal data than the task needs.** A volunteer only needs to know which parcel goes to which street. This code prints everything:\r\n\r\n```python\r\nfamily = {\"code\": \"F-12\", \"street\": \"Palm Street\",\r\n          \"phone\": \"0700-555\", \"health_note\": \"diabetes\"}\r\nprint(family)\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\n{'code': 'F-12', 'street': 'Palm Street', 'phone': '0700-555', 'health_note': 'diabetes'}\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe phone number and health note were not needed and have now been shown to anyone near the screen. **Fixed version:** print only what the task needs.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nfamily = {\"code\": \"F-12\", \"street\": \"Palm Street\",\r\n          \"phone\": \"0700-555\", \"health_note\": \"diabetes\"}\r\nprint(f\"Parcel {family['code']} -> {family['street']}\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nParcel F-12 -> Palm Street\r\n```\r\n\r\n## ✍️ Try It\r\n\r\nAdd four more patients to the audit code, all from `\"Rural North\"`. Run it again. Does the warning still appear? What is the new rural share?\r\n\r\n**Hint:** there will be 6 rural patients out of 10, which is 60%.\r\n\r\n## 📝 Recap\r\n\r\n- Data stands for real people. Collect only what you need, ask for consent and keep it safe (*Amānah*).\r\n- Audit datasets for missing groups, and measure accuracy for each group, not just overall.\r\n- Prefer models that can explain their decisions.\r\n- Check AI answers before trusting or sharing them (*Tabayyun*).\r\n- Refuse harmful uses: deception, surveillance, *Ribā* and *Maysir*.\r\n\r\n## 🏆 Practice\r\n\r\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\r\nWhat does \"data minimisation\" mean? Give one example from a school setting.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\nCollecting **only** the data you really need for the task. Example: a library book-return reminder needs a student code and the book title, not the student's home address or phone number.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\r\nA model was tested on 20 cases: 12 from the city (11 correct) and 8 from villages (4 correct). Work out the overall accuracy and the accuracy for each group. What should the team do?\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```python\r\nprint((11 + 4) / 20 * 100)\r\nprint(round(11 / 12 * 100, 1))\r\nprint(4 / 8 * 100)\r\n```\r\n\r\nOutput:\r\n\r\n```text\r\n75.0\r\n91.7\r\n50.0\r\n```\r\n\r\nOverall 75%, city 91.7%, villages 50%. The team should collect more village data and retest before anyone relies on the model.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\r\nWrite an \"anonymiser\" for a homework-club register.\r\n\r\n1. Start with a list of three dictionaries, each with `name`, `email` and `minutes_studied`.\r\n2. Build a new list where each row has only `student` (codes `S1`, `S2`, `S3`) and `minutes_studied`.\r\n3. Print the new list and the average minutes.\r\n4. Explain in one sentence why the emails were removed.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\r\n\r\n```python\r\nregister = [\r\n    {\"name\": \"Yusuf\", \"email\": \"yusuf@example.org\", \"minutes_studied\": 50},\r\n    {\"name\": \"Maryam\", \"email\": \"maryam@example.org\", \"minutes_studied\": 70},\r\n    {\"name\": \"Idris\", \"email\": \"idris@example.org\", \"minutes_studied\": 30}\r\n]\r\n\r\nsafe = []\r\nnumber = 1\r\ntotal = 0\r\nfor row in register:\r\n    safe.append({\"student\": f\"S{number}\", \"minutes_studied\": row[\"minutes_studied\"]})\r\n    total += row[\"minutes_studied\"]\r\n    number += 1\r\n\r\nprint(safe)\r\nprint(\"Average minutes:\", total / len(safe))\r\n```\r\n\r\nOutput:\r\n\r\n```text\r\n[{'student': 'S1', 'minutes_studied': 50}, {'student': 'S2', 'minutes_studied': 70}, {'student': 'S3', 'minutes_studied': 30}]\r\nAverage minutes: 50.0\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe average does not need anyone's email, so keeping emails would only add risk without any benefit.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n",
  "part-06-ethical-ai-and-capstones/ch15-capstone-community-predictor.md": "# Chapter 15: Capstone Project — Community Relief Predictor 🏆\r\n\r\n## 🌟 Real-Life Story\r\n\r\nIt is a cold winter night at the community centre. Requests for help arrive faster than the volunteers can read them: blankets, warm food, solar lanterns, medical kits. Who should receive help first? The volunteers want to be fast **and** fair. They agree on clear rules, written down so anyone can check them. In this capstone, you will turn those rules into a Python program. *Mabruk* on reaching the final chapter!\r\n\r\n## 🎯 What You Will Learn\r\n\r\n- Plan a project by choosing features and weights for a transparent scoring model.\r\n- Load requests from a CSV file and convert the text into numbers and Booleans.\r\n- Write functions that score, explain and sort the requests.\r\n- Save an audit log as JSON so every decision can be checked later.\r\n- Evaluate the model against past human decisions, and review it for fairness and privacy.\r\n\r\n## 🧭 The Project at a Glance\r\n\r\nYou will build a **Community Relief Priority Tool**. It reads a batch of aid requests, gives each one a priority score, sorts them, and saves a log. Every skill comes from an earlier chapter:\r\n\r\n| Step | What you build | Skills from |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| 1 | Plan features and weights | Ch 11 (features), Ch 14 (ethics) |\r\n| 2 | Load requests from CSV | Ch 9, Ch 10 |\r\n| 3 | Score each request, with reasons | Ch 4, Ch 8 |\r\n| 4 | Turn scores into tiers | Ch 4, Ch 8 |\r\n| 5 | Process and sort the batch | Ch 5, Ch 6, Ch 7 |\r\n| 6 | Save a JSON audit log | Ch 10 |\r\n| 7 | Evaluate against past decisions | Ch 13 |\r\n| 8 | Ethics review | Ch 14 |\r\n\r\n> 🧪 **Honest note**: this is a simplified teaching model with made-up requests. The weights are chosen by people, not learned from data. In real relief work, the tool would only **suggest** an order, and trained volunteers would make the final decisions.\r\n\r\nType each step into the Python Console. Each step adds to the code of the steps before it, so keep everything in one program.\r\n\r\n## 📐 Step 1: Plan the Features and Weights\r\n\r\nFirst, decide which facts (features) matter and how many points each one adds. The volunteers agreed on these:\r\n\r\n| Feature | Why it matters | Points |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| No heating at home | Cold is the biggest danger tonight | +40 |\r\n| Infant in the family | Babies are most at risk from cold | +35 |\r\n| Elderly member | Older people are also at high risk | +25 |\r\n| More than 5 people | A bigger household needs more supplies | +15 |\r\n\r\nThen decide the **tiers** (priority groups):\r\n\r\n| Score | Tier |\r\n| :--- | :--- |\r\n| 70 or more | Tier 1: immediate dispatch |\r\n| 40 to 69 | Tier 2: same-day delivery |\r\n| under 40 | Tier 3: standard delivery |\r\n\r\nNotice what is **not** in the plan: names, phone numbers, religion or nationality. They are not needed to decide who is coldest, so we do not collect them. Each request has only a code, like `REQ-801` (*Amānah*).\r\n\r\n## 📥 Step 2: Load the Requests from CSV\r\n\r\nRequests arrive as a CSV file. To make the project self-contained, we first write a sample file, then read it back. CSV values are always text (Chapter 10), so we convert them: `int()` for numbers, and `== \"yes\"` to turn `\"yes\"`/`\"no\"` into `True`/`False`.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nimport csv\r\nimport json\r\n\r\nwith open(\"requests.csv\", \"w\", newline=\"\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\r\n    f.write(\"request_id,members,infants,elderly,heating\\n\")\r\n    f.write(\"REQ-801,6,yes,yes,no\\n\")\r\n    f.write(\"REQ-802,3,no,no,yes\\n\")\r\n    f.write(\"REQ-803,4,no,yes,no\\n\")\r\n    f.write(\"REQ-804,7,yes,no,yes\\n\")\r\n    f.write(\"REQ-805,2,no,yes,yes\\n\")\r\n    f.write(\"REQ-806,5,yes,no,no\\n\")\r\n\r\ndef load_requests(filename):\r\n    \"\"\"Read the CSV file and return a list of clean dictionaries.\"\"\"\r\n    requests = []\r\n    with open(filename, \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\r\n        for row in csv.DictReader(f):\r\n            requests.append({\r\n                \"id\": row[\"request_id\"],\r\n                \"members\": int(row[\"members\"]),\r\n                \"has_infants\": row[\"infants\"] == \"yes\",\r\n                \"has_elderly\": row[\"elderly\"] == \"yes\",\r\n                \"has_heating\": row[\"heating\"] == \"yes\"\r\n            })\r\n    return requests\r\n\r\nrequests = load_requests(\"requests.csv\")\r\nprint(len(requests), \"requests loaded\")\r\nprint(requests[0])\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\n6 requests loaded\r\n{'id': 'REQ-801', 'members': 6, 'has_infants': True, 'has_elderly': True, 'has_heating': False}\r\n```\r\n\r\n## 🧮 Step 3: Score Each Request, with Reasons\r\n\r\nThe scoring function follows the plan in Step 1 exactly. It also collects a list of **reasons**, so every score can be explained (Chapter 14).\r\n\r\n```python\r\ndef score_request(req):\r\n    \"\"\"Return (score, reasons) for one request.\"\"\"\r\n    score = 0\r\n    reasons = []\r\n    if not req[\"has_heating\"]:\r\n        score += 40\r\n        reasons.append(\"no heating\")\r\n    if req[\"has_infants\"]:\r\n        score += 35\r\n        reasons.append(\"infant\")\r\n    if req[\"has_elderly\"]:\r\n        score += 25\r\n        reasons.append(\"elderly member\")\r\n    if req[\"members\"] > 5:\r\n        score += 15\r\n        reasons.append(\"large household\")\r\n    return score, reasons\r\n\r\nprint(score_request(requests[0]))\r\nprint(score_request(requests[1]))\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\n(115, ['no heating', 'infant', 'elderly member', 'large household'])\r\n(0, [])\r\n```\r\n\r\nCheck the first one by hand: 40 + 35 + 25 + 15 = 115. The second request has no risk factors, so it scores 0 with no reasons.\r\n\r\n## 🚦 Step 4: Turn Scores into Tiers\r\n\r\n```python\r\ndef tier_for(score):\r\n    \"\"\"Return the priority tier for a score.\"\"\"\r\n    if score >= 70:\r\n        return \"Tier 1: immediate\"\r\n    elif score >= 40:\r\n        return \"Tier 2: same day\"\r\n    return \"Tier 3: standard\"\r\n\r\nprint(tier_for(115))\r\nprint(tier_for(50))\r\nprint(tier_for(25))\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nTier 1: immediate\r\nTier 2: same day\r\nTier 3: standard\r\n```\r\n\r\nTest the borders too: `tier_for(70)` and `tier_for(40)` should give Tier 1 and Tier 2. Testing the edges is where many bugs hide.\r\n\r\n## 🔁 Step 5: Process and Sort the Batch\r\n\r\nNow loop over every request, build a **record** (a result dictionary) for each, and sort the records so the highest score comes first.\r\n\r\n`.sort()` can take a **key**: a function that tells it **what to sort by**. We pass the function's name, `get_score`, without brackets. Python calls it on each record and sorts by the numbers it returns.\r\n\r\n```python\r\ndispatches = []\r\nfor req in requests:\r\n    score, reasons = score_request(req)\r\n    dispatches.append({\r\n        \"id\": req[\"id\"],\r\n        \"score\": score,\r\n        \"tier\": tier_for(score),\r\n        \"reasons\": reasons\r\n    })\r\n\r\ndef get_score(record):\r\n    return record[\"score\"]\r\n\r\ndispatches.sort(key=get_score, reverse=True)\r\n\r\nprint(\"=\" * 40)\r\nprint(\"COMMUNITY RELIEF DISPATCH ORDER\")\r\nprint(\"=\" * 40)\r\nfor d in dispatches:\r\n    print(f\"{d['id']}  score {d['score']}  {d['tier']}\")\r\n    print(f\"   because: {', '.join(d['reasons'])}\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\n========================================\r\nCOMMUNITY RELIEF DISPATCH ORDER\r\n========================================\r\nREQ-801  score 115  Tier 1: immediate\r\n   because: no heating, infant, elderly member, large household\r\nREQ-806  score 75  Tier 1: immediate\r\n   because: no heating, infant\r\nREQ-803  score 65  Tier 2: same day\r\n   because: no heating, elderly member\r\nREQ-804  score 50  Tier 2: same day\r\n   because: infant, large household\r\nREQ-805  score 25  Tier 3: standard\r\n   because: elderly member\r\nREQ-802  score 0  Tier 3: standard\r\n   because: \r\n```\r\n\r\n`\", \".join(list)` glues a list of strings into one string, with `\", \"` between the items. When the list is empty, it gives an empty string, which is why REQ-802 shows nothing after \"because:\".\r\n\r\n## 🗂️ Step 6: Save a JSON Audit Log\r\n\r\nAn **audit log** is a saved record of what the program decided and why. Anyone can check it later. That makes the system accountable.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nlog = {\r\n    \"batch\": \"winter-night-1\",\r\n    \"rules\": \"heating 40, infant 35, elderly 25, large household 15\",\r\n    \"dispatches\": dispatches\r\n}\r\n\r\nwith open(\"dispatch_log.json\", \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\r\n    json.dump(log, f, indent=2)\r\n\r\nwith open(\"dispatch_log.json\", \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as f:\r\n    saved = json.load(f)\r\n\r\nprint(\"Saved\", len(saved[\"dispatches\"]), \"decisions\")\r\nprint(\"First in line:\", saved[\"dispatches\"][0][\"id\"])\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nSaved 6 decisions\r\nFirst in line: REQ-801\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe log stores codes and reasons only. No names or contact details are written to disk.\r\n\r\n## 🧪 Step 7: Evaluate Against Past Decisions\r\n\r\nLast winter, experienced volunteers sorted eight requests by hand. A good tool should mostly **agree** with them. This is like the test set in Chapter 13: the model never saw these cases while we chose the weights. (The data is made up.)\r\n\r\n```python\r\npast_cases = [\r\n    ({\"id\": \"P1\", \"members\": 4, \"has_infants\": True, \"has_elderly\": False, \"has_heating\": False}, \"Tier 1: immediate\"),\r\n    ({\"id\": \"P2\", \"members\": 2, \"has_infants\": False, \"has_elderly\": False, \"has_heating\": True}, \"Tier 3: standard\"),\r\n    ({\"id\": \"P3\", \"members\": 6, \"has_infants\": False, \"has_elderly\": True, \"has_heating\": True}, \"Tier 1: immediate\"),\r\n    ({\"id\": \"P4\", \"members\": 3, \"has_infants\": False, \"has_elderly\": True, \"has_heating\": False}, \"Tier 2: same day\"),\r\n    ({\"id\": \"P5\", \"members\": 8, \"has_infants\": False, \"has_elderly\": False, \"has_heating\": True}, \"Tier 2: same day\"),\r\n    ({\"id\": \"P6\", \"members\": 5, \"has_infants\": True, \"has_elderly\": True, \"has_heating\": True}, \"Tier 2: same day\"),\r\n    ({\"id\": \"P7\", \"members\": 1, \"has_infants\": False, \"has_elderly\": False, \"has_heating\": False}, \"Tier 2: same day\"),\r\n    ({\"id\": \"P8\", \"members\": 7, \"has_infants\": True, \"has_elderly\": True, \"has_heating\": False}, \"Tier 1: immediate\")\r\n]\r\n\r\nagree = 0\r\nfor case, volunteer_tier in past_cases:\r\n    score, reasons = score_request(case)\r\n    model_tier = tier_for(score)\r\n    if model_tier == volunteer_tier:\r\n        agree += 1\r\n    else:\r\n        print(f\"{case['id']}: model says {model_tier}, volunteers said {volunteer_tier}\")\r\n\r\nprint(f\"Agreement: {agree} of {len(past_cases)} ({agree / len(past_cases) * 100}%)\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nP3: model says Tier 2: same day, volunteers said Tier 1: immediate\r\nP5: model says Tier 3: standard, volunteers said Tier 2: same day\r\nAgreement: 6 of 8 (75.0%)\r\n```\r\n\r\nStudy each disagreement instead of hiding it. Here, both disagreements are big families (P3 with 6 people, P5 with 8). The volunteers placed them higher than the model did. Perhaps \"large household\" deserves more than 15 points. Changing a weight and testing again is how you improve a model honestly. Just remember Chapter 13: if you tune the weights on these eight cases, you need fresh cases for the next honest test.\r\n\r\n## 🛡️ Step 8: Ethics Review\r\n\r\nBefore anyone uses the tool, go through this checklist:\r\n\r\n- [ ] **Privacy:** only the data needed for the decision is collected and saved.\r\n- [ ] **Transparency:** every score comes with its reasons; the rules are written in the log.\r\n- [ ] **Fairness:** no feature is about race, religion, nationality or wealth.\r\n- [ ] **Human control:** volunteers can change the order, and the tool never refuses help to anyone.\r\n- [ ] **Honesty:** users are told it is a simple rule-based helper, not an all-knowing AI.\r\n\r\n## 🚀 Where to Go from Here\r\n\r\nYou have completed the **Python & Machine Learning Foundations** book. You can now:\r\n\r\n- write clean, modular Python with functions, lists, dictionaries and files;\r\n- describe datasets, features, labels, models, accuracy and overfitting;\r\n- build tools that are transparent, fair and protective of people's data.\r\n\r\nKeep practising in the Python Console. Try new made-up datasets from science, energy or your school library, and use your skills to serve your family, school and community.\r\n\r\n## ⚠️ Common Mistake\r\n\r\n**Treating the text `\"no\"` from a CSV as `False`.** Any non-empty string counts as `True` in an `if`, even the word `\"no\"`. So this code says a family has heating when the file says `\"no\"`:\r\n\r\n```python\r\nrow = {\"heating\": \"no\"}\r\nif row[\"heating\"]:\r\n    print(\"Has heating\")\r\nelse:\r\n    print(\"No heating\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nHas heating\r\n```\r\n\r\nWrong! A family without heating would lose 40 priority points. **Fixed version:** compare the text, as in Step 2.\r\n\r\n```python\r\nrow = {\"heating\": \"no\"}\r\nhas_heating = row[\"heating\"] == \"yes\"\r\nif has_heating:\r\n    print(\"Has heating\")\r\nelse:\r\n    print(\"No heating\")\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Output:**\r\n\r\n```text\r\nNo heating\r\n```\r\n\r\n## ✍️ Try It\r\n\r\nAdd a new request to the CSV in Step 2: `REQ-807,9,no,no,no`. Before you run the program, work out its score and tier by hand. Then run it and check where it appears in the dispatch order.\r\n\r\n**Hint:** no heating (40) + large household (15) = 55, so Tier 2.\r\n\r\n## 📝 Recap\r\n\r\n- A good project starts with a plan: features, weights, tiers, and what data **not** to collect.\r\n- Convert CSV text carefully: `int()` for numbers and `== \"yes\"` for Booleans.\r\n- Small functions (`load_requests`, `score_request`, `tier_for`) make the program easy to test and explain.\r\n- `.sort(key=function, reverse=True)` sorts records by any value you choose.\r\n- Save a JSON audit log, compare with human decisions, and review privacy and fairness before use.\r\n\r\n## 🏆 Practice\r\n\r\n### 🟢 Level 1: Recall\r\nWhy does the project store request codes like `REQ-801` instead of family names? Name the principle.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\nNames are not needed to decide priority, so collecting them would only add risk. Keeping only the data you need protects people's privacy. This is **data minimisation**, part of *Amānah* (trust).\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🟡 Level 2: Apply\r\nWithout running the code, find the score and tier of a request with 3 members, an infant, no elderly member, and **no** heating.\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show answer</summary>\r\n\r\nNo heating 40 + infant 35 = **75**. 75 is 70 or more, so it is **Tier 1: immediate**. (3 members is not more than 5, so no extra points.)\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n\r\n### 🔴 Level 3: Challenge\r\nImprove the tool with a new feature and a summary.\r\n\r\n1. Add a `medical` column to the CSV (`yes`/`no`) for a family member who needs regular medicine. Give each request a value.\r\n2. Load it as a Boolean in `load_requests`.\r\n3. In `score_request`, add **+30** points and the reason `\"medical need\"` when it is `True`.\r\n4. After sorting, count how many requests are in each tier with a dictionary, and print the counts.\r\n5. Re-run Step 7. Did the agreement change? (The past cases have no `medical` key, so use `req.get(\"medical\", False)`.)\r\n\r\n<details>\r\n<summary>Show one possible answer</summary>\r\n\r\nThe key changes:\r\n\r\n```python\r\n# In load_requests, inside the dictionary:\r\n\"medical\": row[\"medical\"] == \"yes\"\r\n\r\n# In score_request:\r\nif req.get(\"medical\", False):\r\n    score += 30\r\n    reasons.append(\"medical need\")\r\n\r\n# After sorting:\r\ntier_counts = {}\r\nfor d in dispatches:\r\n    tier_counts[d[\"tier\"]] = tier_counts.get(d[\"tier\"], 0) + 1\r\nfor tier, count in tier_counts.items():\r\n    print(f\"{tier}: {count} request(s)\")\r\n```\r\n\r\nUsing `.get(\"medical\", False)` means old records without the new column still work. The Step 7 agreement stays the same, because none of the past cases has a medical need.\r\n\r\n</details>\r\n",
  "README.md": "# Python & Machine Learning for Junior Coders 🐍🤖\r\n\r\nWelcome to the official **Python & Applied Machine Learning Textbook** of the Junior Coders curriculum!\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🎯 Course Overview\r\n\r\nPython is the world's most popular programming language for Artificial Intelligence, Data Science, and automation. Known for its elegant, English-like syntax and vast ecosystem, Python empowers students to move from fundamental computational thinking to building intelligent systems that learn from data.\r\n\r\nIn this textbook, students will:\r\n\r\n1. **Master Core Python**: Variables, control flow, functions, collections, and clean file I/O.\r\n2. **Think Like a Data Scientist**: Load, parse, clean, and analyze structured datasets (CSV & JSON).\r\n3. **Understand Machine Learning from First Principles**: Learn how models discover patterns in data without explicit hardcoded rules.\r\n4. **Build Supervised Learning Classifiers**: Train decision boundaries, evaluate accuracy, and detect overfitting.\r\n5. **Practice Ethical AI & Digital Stewardship (*Amānah*)**: Protect privacy, audit algorithms for bias, and build technology for societal benefit (*'Ilm Nāfi'*).\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🗺️ Curriculum Roadmap\r\n\r\n| Part | Focus | Chapters |\r\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\r\n| **Part 1** | Python Foundations & Syntax | Ch 1 - Ch 3 |\r\n| **Part 2** | Control Flow, Logic & Loops | Ch 4 - Ch 5 |\r\n| **Part 3** | Data Structures & Functions | Ch 6 - Ch 8 |\r\n| **Part 4** | Data Processing & File Systems | Ch 9 - Ch 10 |\r\n| **Part 5** | Machine Learning Fundamentals | Ch 11 - Ch 13 |\r\n| **Part 6** | Ethical AI & Capstones | Ch 14 - Ch 15 |\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n## 🌟 Islamic Ethical Framework (*Amānah & Iḥsān*)\r\n\r\nAll lessons in this curriculum adhere to our core ethical standards:\r\n\r\n- **Data Privacy as a Sacred Trust (*Amānah*)**: User data and sensitive records must be protected. Never scrape or train models on private personal data without explicit informed consent.\r\n- **Beneficial Knowledge (*'Ilm Nāfi'*)**: Build models that solve real human challenges—such as agricultural plant disease detection, clean energy monitoring, and equitable charity distribution.\r\n- **Strictly Halal Contexts**: No models for loan interest/usury (*Ribā*), sports betting/gambling (*Maysir*), deepfakes, or intrusive surveillance.\r\n- **Transparency & Accountability**: Strive for explainable AI where decisions can be understood and audited rather than harmful black boxes.\r\n"
};
